In article <2dg0k6INN43s@tonto.ksu.ksu.edu>, strat@tonto.ksu.ksu.edu (Steve Davis) writes:
>I disagree.  There will always be a PC class and a Workstation class,
>simply because there are different audiences for each type of machine.
>Joe Schmoe doesn't need or want many features available in today's
>workstations.  The typical workstation user probably won't settle for a
>machine marketed to the masses.

The question is, what is the difference in the feature list? Only in
size, not in contents. It used to be, and certainly is for the most
part, that workstations are machines designed for networking, using a
multiuser multitasking OS, with a large graphics display.
>You are confused.  The difference between a PC class machine and a
>workstation class machine has nothing to do with what operating systems
>they run, or what type of video they're capable of producing.  A $10,000
>SGI will always out perform a $3,000 IBM.  It's practically a law of
>nature.

The things is, up until now, the differences between workstations and
PCs has been primarily architectural, in the sense of what operating
systems they run, and the type of video they produce. The difference
these days is in the difference that the PC will run with less speed but
with less cost as well, while the workstation is purchased more for pure
performance. There is no dividing characteristic between PC's and
workstations. You can say "fast CPU's" but how fast is fast in this case?
The same goes for lots of RAM and disk.

-- 
