In article <2dg2v8$hkg@inews.intel.com>,
Joe Bennett <jbennett@frx146.intel.com> wrote:
..
>I really, really wish people would stop saying clock rate == performance.
>In the Alpha, less work is done per clock cycle, so more clock cycles are
>necessary to achieve the same result, so you need a higher clock rate to
>achieve that result.  The interesting thing to note is that when the Alpha
>is slowed down such that you only have to do a divide by 2 or 3 on the clock
>to get a manageable PC-style motherboard design, the performance isn't that
>impressive, and is why the PowerPC is stealing all of Alpha's thunder about
>a "RISC PC".

Of course clock rate != performance.  Clock rate is, however, an important
contributor to performance.  Chip performance comes from a number of
attributes.  Instructions issued per cycle, cycles per unit time, and "work"
done per instruction figure heavily into the overall performance of the
machine.  A chip which does more real work per instruction will be
faster with fewer instruction issues than a machine which does less per
instruction.  That is why the pentium offers better performance than a
dual issue alpha at a given clock rate.  It takes significantly fewer
instructions to achieve a given effect on pentium than it does on Alpha.
The thing is, RISC architectures like Alpha are easier to scale up in terms
of clock speed than CISC architectures like Pentium (or VAX).  That means
that the clock speed on the Alpha can increase faster than the clock speed
on Pentium.  Thus, any closing of the overall performance gap between the two
processors must come from somewhere other than Pentium closing the clock
rate gap.

The thing is, Alpha has an advantage there, too.  The instruction set was
designed with multiple issue and heavy pipelining in mind.  Alpha implemen-
tations capable of issuing four instructions simultaneously have already
been made, and there is nothing in the architecture to make it difficult
to issue even more instructions simultaneously.  The designers of any Pentium
follow-on will have to scrounge really hard to improve its instruction issue
rate.

The one area where Pentium has an advantage over any RISC architecture is
in memory bandwidth.  Since it takes fewer instructions to run a program
on a Pentium, it doesn't have to bring as much memory into the cache to
perform a given amount of work.  That means it will be less constrained
by needing to fetch instructions.  Effectively, though, that means it won't
benefit as well from faster memory systems as the Alpha will.  A lot of
the performance hits on the Alpha come from the chip being too fast for
the memory subsystem, so as the performance of cheap memory systems improve,
Alpha should see a proportionately better increase in performance.  Right
now the situation works against Alpha, but that should change.

Now, Alpha vs. PowerPC is another story, since the PowerPC design has some
important features in common with the Alpha architecture, but that's another
story.  PowerPC can definitely give Alpha a good run for its money.  I
don't think either architecture has an inherent advantage.  That battle will
probably be decided more by market factors (and management decisions) than
because of any technical differences.

- Brad
