The distinctions between PC and workstation architectures are becoming
meaningless due to the following changes in PC architecture:

CPU: The Pentium CPU provides performance that is greater than a
SparcStation 10.  ISPEC performance of Pentium exceeds Sparc Viking-40
and PowerPC 601-66.  FSPEC performance of Pentium is major leap over
486 architecture, Pentium provides 5-7x FSPEC performance of the
486DX2-66.  While Pentium's FPU performance is not equivalent to the

fastest RISC CPUs, it makes up most of the gap. FP instructions are
less than 10% of most workstation software, so FPU performance is not
very significant overall. So the performance is now on par.

Graphics & I/O Performance: The arrival of the PCI bus means that the
I/O and graphics performance on the PC will match that of most
workstations.  PCI allows system bus performance at 33MHZ vs. 8MHZ for
ISA, enabling fast disk access and very quick graphics performance
that rivals the leading workstations.  Graphics accelerator cards with
2MB of VRAM are now common in PCs. Due to these changes, PCI based PCs
match the leading workstations in terms of I/O and graphics. In fact,
DEC Alpha and PowerPC are both adopting PCI architectures for their
next generations of workstations, so there is no longer much
difference here.

Operating System & Memory Model: The arrival of Windows-NT means that
PCs will now have a robust, demand paged virtual memory O/S that
supports multitasking and multiprocessing. So the limitations of the
DOS O/S are now disappering, and you have an O/S for the PC that
rivals the best capabilities of UNIX.  Those that want to run UNIX can
now choose from Solaris, NextStep, and UnixWare, all of which run
quite well on PC platforms.  Essentially, the same O/S capabilities
that were available on workstations are available on PCs today.

Applications Software: Many of the best-selling workstation software
programs are now available on PCs.  Virtually all the rest are in the
process of porting to Win-NT or a PC-based UNIX O/S.  So the
distinction between applications is vanishing too, with PCs now
provding workstation apps in addition to all the PC applications that
are available.

In sum, most of the architectural distinctions between PCs and
workstations are already gone or will vanish early next year.
Meanwhile, PCs will continue to provide a significant system
price/performance advantage over workstations, because their high
volumes provide economies of scale that drive costs down.  Today, you
can buy a fully configured Pentium based PC, with a PCI bus, graphics
accelerator with 2MB VRAM, Win-NT, 32MB of memory, 500MB hard disk,
and 17" monitor for around $5000, if you shop carefully. By next year
that configuration will probably hit $4000 or less. It will be very
hard to find workstations that deliver this kind of system
price/performance.

Given this situation, the two leading workstation market research
vendors, IDC and Dataquest, both project that the PC and workstation
markets will be merging over the next few years. IDC calls this new
market the "Power Desktop", and believes that PCs will have the lion's
share of this market.  Dataquest has come to a similar conclusion.

- S. Kaufman
   




  











