In article <AApm8zi8F5@mike.fian.msk.su>, michael@mike.fian.msk.su (Michael V. Khaletskij) writes:
> We have in our institute old VAX-11/780
> and it uses NEC disk drives D2363
> Is it possible to find any new drives to replace them ?
> May be of smaller weight ?

  It depends on availability of money and possibly export restrictions on high-
end disk drives (I don't think of disk drives as strategic, but the papers I
get with my drives all say "may not be exported without license". Perhaps that
is just standard text).

  Anyway, a current replacement for your drive would be a SCSI-2 disk drive
like the Seagate ST41650N (1.35Gb formatted) or the ST42400N (2.1Gb formatted).
You could use a CMD 720 controller in the 780's Unibus to run these. The disks
are 5.25" full-height units and are much smaller, faster, and use less power
than your old SMD drive. Also, these new drives are warranted for five years.
The ST42400 costs about US $1650 at this time. I believe the CMD controller is
about US $1000.

> What is this standard ? I didn't hear if such standards
> are available now. We use these old disks with Emulex
> controller which emulates UDA50 for the system.

  There are still some SMD drives around, but very few are being made today.
SCSI now outperforms the SMD drives and is more standardized (with SMD you
have to worry about the data rate, whether signals are on the A or B cables,
etc., while SCSI is more "plug and play"). You may be able to get a drive
like a Sabre 9720, but it's likely to cost a lot more than a SCSI disk, and
may cost more than the (bigger) SCSI disk and controller together. And then
there's the shipping expense for a big, heavy disk drive.

> Do you know what company can sell us such kind of disks ?

  Here in the US there are many companies that sell the disks and controllers.
I don't know how many of them are prepared to export to you. If you want me
to research this for you, send me email.

  Lastly, unless you have unusual peripherals on your 780, you might want to
consider replacing it with a smaller, faster system. For about US $1000 you
can get a MicroVAX III (KA650 CPU, BA23, 2 x MS650 memory) which is about 2.5
times faster than the 780. If you're going to replace the disk controller,
this might be an ideal time to switch over, again assuming that export rules
allow it. Given that the US can send R4000-based RISC systems to China, one
would expect that it's possible, but you never know...

