In article <62024@ogicse.ogi.edu>, fanty@cse.ogi.edu (Mark Fanty) writes:

|>What are the best options for a Alpha/OSF file server with throughput
|>comparable to a four processor SPARCcenter 1000?  We need to upgrade
|>our Sun file server (it's maxed out in disk capacity). We like our two 
|>DEC 3000/400 workstations a lot and would like an Alpha server, but
|>our Sun file server handles our NFS demands very well.  I would like to be
|>reassured by seeing some NFS comparisons or hearing from people
|>who put a heavy IO load on a comparably priced alpha.  Let's say there
|>are two users accessing files, some of them > 100MB and two tape backups
|>in progress.  Does is handle it no sweat?

All the new systems will do quite nicely, compared to the SPARCcenter 1000.  It
all depends on what you need in terms of storage.  You SHOULD NOT buy a 3000/400
as a fileserver since it has been pretty much replaced by the 3000/600 at about
the same price, but with SUBSTANTIAL increases in performance, due to bigger
cache, faster chip and faster internal SCSI.

System          3000/400        3000/600        3000/500        3000/800

Integral SCSI     5MB/s          10MB/s           5MB/s          10MB/s
Secondary Cache   512K            2MB             512K            2MB
MHz		  133		 175		  150		 200
SPECint92         74.8           114.1            84.4           130.2
SPECfp92          112.5          162.1           127.7           184.0

Be sure that you order prestoserve for WHICHEVER system you get; makes a big
difference of NFS performance.

|>If I seem to be worrying a lot, we had an inadequate file server once (a Sun
|>desktop workstation) and it was terrible.  I just don't know how the Alpha's 
|>blazing number crunching will translate into NFS performance..

The blazing CPU does NOT translate into superior NFS performance, but fast
disks and prestoserve AND a hot CPU *DO* make for good NFS performance.  And,
if you decide to go to FDDI to improve your throughput, our FDDI has been 
clocked at 93MB/s, so it can take alot of heat.  You can also hang IPI disks
off the turbochannel with a Velocitor controller.

If you want higher expansion capabilities, you might want to look at either
the 4000/610 or the 4000/710.  They can take more memory, more disk and support
all the stuff I mentioned above for the 3000, plus you can add another 
processor.  If you want to go even higher in expandability(adding more
processors/memory/disks) you can go with the 6000 series.  OSF/1 doesn't support
SMP *yet* but we have already announced that it will be out in 1994.

You can find lots more detail in the Systems and Options Catalogue on 
gatekeeper.dec.com.

If you want to try out a 4000, you can get a free account on axposf.pa.dec.com
(rlogin axposf.pa.dec.com -l guest; no password, hit return and answer ?s).
It is currently a 4000/610 and will be upgraded to a 710 shortly.

