-- 
The SISCO version being used is the OSI/MMS version, this uses the
MAP User Groups proposal for an MMS Application Programming Interface,
MMSI.  The MMSI API is a low level programming interface and requires
strict adherence to it structures etc...  The original MMS-EASE API
is somewhat more flexible and allows programming at a higher level,
DEComni goes a stage further and is an even higher level API.  The 
problems here actually lie in the example programs provided by OSI/MMS,
there seems to be little problem when the OSI/MMS Client application
runs and makes requests to DEComni.  However, the OSI/MMS Server 
example application is not very robust, after all it is only an
example as is not intended as the basis for a serious MMS program.

I made a visit to IWB, it's near my home, took along a VAX/VMS system
that I use here and performed lots of tracing too see if we could
detemine the problems, I was also running DEComni.  We did transport
traces, presentation traces and MMS PDU traces, we also put the MMS
PDUs through an OSS ASN1 application that suppors the MMS syntax.
We found the the DEComni ULTRIX MMS decoder was having problems because
the MMS PDU responses from the OSI/MMS server application, were either
incorrect or the server replied with an Abort.  We also noted that the
demo would also corrupt previously working functions, we actually
managed to perform a name list operation and read variable operation,
all the rest were wrong.

So basically its down to problems in the example OSI/MMS server application,
the guys from SISCO have faxed us a fix for one problem.  But really we need
to think again and either wait for a fully test/error free server example,
use an alternative, i.e. the original MMS-EASE or maybe EASYmap from Procos
on the PC.  The last resort is to write/modify an application from scratch,
but considering the low level nature of the MMSI API and the ease at which
one can introduce errors, I'd say its a lost cause.

If anybody out there would like to volenteer a usable OSI/MMS application,
that has preferably been through an inter-operability and/or conformance
test like DEComni has (Contact Daniel Habib, ACERLI, [33]1-46383508) and
that runs on OS2 then I'm sure IWB will be very appreciative.

Apart from that the tips from Christian IPK Berlin are valid, some MMS
implementations are unable to handle the IS version of MMS.  DEComni supports
both DIS and IS, but then most implementations do.  The length problem for
Identify is a known feature from the DEComni demo application OmniView, it
looks as though some buffer lengths were hard-coded and were insufficient
for the longer ids.  However, a user written application would work just
fine.

Chris

