In article <hermann.754068876@danetis>, hermann@is.danet.de (Hermann Kneissel) writes:
|> 
|>   but the text file should contain some primitive formatting commands
|>   for example to print some words bold or italic.
|> Is there a simple way to do this, may be some public domain converter
|> from text to ddif, or is there a document describing the ddif format ?

  If you want to put formatting information into your text file, you must use a
known format.  DECwrite pokes around a bit in raw ASCII files and tries to do the
right thing.  I doubt if that means that it handles any sort of tagging to indicate
that text should be bolded or italicized.

  Therefore, if you want formatting information in your text file, you must use
an existing format such as SGML or a similar text-based markup language for
documents.  Those formats provide mechanisms for you to put 'commands' into your
text to indicate how it should be formatted.  Once you have formatted your document
with the appropriate tags to follow SGML (or whatever), you then use one of the
CDA converters (or import the document via the DECwrite File menu - I'm out of date,
but that's where it should be).  You may simply find it easier to go into the
document after DECwrite has included it and bold and italicize words there...

[Former DECcie on DECwrite V1.0, original author of text include function]

