In article <kanefsky-251193000604@kanefsky.halcyon.com>, kanefsky@halcyon.com (Steve Kanefsky) writes:
>This is silly.  There will always be a class of machines ("workstations")
>that will outperform the machines that most individuals can afford
>("personal computers" or "PCs").  The only way this would not be the case
>is if computers become more powerful than anyone needs them to be, and I
>don't ever see *that* happening.  And by "outperform" I don't simply mean
>running benchmarks.  I mean entering, processing, viewing, storing, and
>outputting data.

The point of the original article was that the differences were becoming
more quantitative than qualitative. In past years, you could define a
workstation by the fact that it had built in ethernet, ran Unix or some
other preemptive multitasking OS, had a large graphic display, etc.

At this point we're getting to the stage where those features are appearing
in the PC end. Yes, there is a difference between a workstation and a PC,
if one looks at either end of the spectrum. The middle ground however, gets
especially blurry.

The main difference between a PC and a workstation is that the former works
on a price/performance ratio, while the latter sells mainly on performance.
At least in the near future, with the commentary in the original article,
showing that the feature list between PC OS's and workstation OS's are
starting to converge.

All your differences between a workstation and a PC are defined by
differences in degree, rather than any qualitative difference. Yes, a
faster CPU. Yes, more memory. Yes, more hard disk. You can set up an
arbitrary breakpoint and say "below this, all are PC's, above it there
are workstations" but that's an arbitrary line. There used to be much
bigger differences between PC's and workstations, and those differences
are starting to go.

