>Pentium may be on par with Sun, but the high-end Alpha PC's (by Carerra,
>anyway) are twice as fast as the Pentium. 

So far the benchmarks for these systems have not impressed me,
considering price/performance. BYTE put a 100 MHz R4400 about 6% faster
than a 60 MHz pentium for floating point, and a 150 MHz alpha (with 512K
write-back cache and 128-bit bus) about 1.5 times faster. The BYTE
savage benchmark in Excel on a 150 MHz R4400 was 25% slower than Excel
under win 3.1 on a 60 MHz pentium. The French rag PC Expert looked at a
DEC alpha 150 MHz, and found it scored 15% higher on CPU, and 40% lower
on graphics than a 60 MHz pentium.

I'm sure that with well-optimized code, the alphas can do better, but
judging by the difficulty BYTE had getting simple benchmarks to
optimize, the proccess may not be that simple. We have two alphas at
work, and they do an outstanding job compared to the vaxes they
replaced, but I suspect they have much better buses/peripherals compared
to the low-end alpha PC's.



