In article <3525@niktow.canisius.edu>,
Greg Pavlov <pavlov@niktow.canisius.edu> wrote:
...
>  But the Alpha accomplishes this by running at twice the clock rate and more.
>  IBM accomplishes the same thing by generally adhering to "RISC" concepts but
>  implementing them via a complex chip set (POWER and POWER2).  MIPS does the
>  same via clock-doubling.  HP, for one reason or another, has either chosen
>  not to or failed to implement on-chip cache.
>
>  So on the surface, it appears to me that as each architecture is pushed to 
>  higher performance, distinct problems will have to be faced and overcome. 
>  HP may be an exception - the cache "problem" may not be a problem.  Power PC
>  may be an exception as well, at least for the time being.

This is exactly where the problem lies with Pentium.  As you state later,
Pentium was a major accomplishment *given its constraints*.  Those constraints
are very severe, however.  I will be extremely surprised if I *ever* see a
Pentium running over 200 MHz, while the Alpha can already run well over 300
MHz.  The Alpha architecture is designed to work with high clock speeds.
Also, future implementations of Alpha will only increase in peak performance
per MHz, since the architecture is also designed so that it is easy to in-
crease internal parallelism.  The constraints faced by the designers of
Pentium made it impossible to build that kind of scalability into the design.

The thing that's neat about Alpha relative to most (all? I don't know if
the PowerPC uses condition flags or exact FP exceptions) of the other
hot architectures is that it was designed to be one of the best performers
today, and to scale up in performance well into the future.  The marketing
hype about it being the only architecture you'll need for the next 25 years
is probably just hype, but the architecture definitely has at least 10-15
years of substantial performance improvements in it.  The scaling problems
that Alpha is likely to face will all have to do with its speed relative
to the system it lives in.  Memory and I/O bandwidth will more and more be the
main constraints on its performance.  Most other architectures will run
into progressively more difficult design problems as the designers try to
get faster implementations.  Eventually, those architectures will have to
undergo major revisions to keep up with the pace Alpha was designed to set.
Now, it may turn out that those future architecture revisions incorporate
some as-yet-to-be-developed approaches to making performance even easier
to improve, and that they may outstrip the Alpha, but Alpha is the best
of the current batch as far as performance scalability.

Of course, it's perfectly reasonable to argue that scalability is irrelevant
if you're deciding what to put on your desktop today or tomorrow, but you
were making it sound like Alpha was cheating by running at a higher clock
rate, when the higher clock rate is in many ways a symptom of its high
performance as much as a cause.

- Brad
