In article <CH48J2.7F4@newsflash.concordia.ca>, cliff@cs.concordia.ca (GROSSNER cliff) writes:
|>    I have what I think is a rather simple question. I would like to know
|> what is required to hook X-Terminals to a VAX computer. The VAX is a 
|> 6510, and a 3200, both are running VMS. The version of X that were
|> are currently supporting is X11R5 on our unix stations. Can we connect
|> the VAX computers to these X-terminals? Do we need any special software
|> for our VAX computers? Where can we get such software? Does anyone
|> know of a place where they have this running.
|> 

We have NCD X terminals running off a VAXstation 3200 running VMS.
Basically, you need to have all necessary boot and font files stored
on the VAX system and your NCD configured to use VMS-style filenames
and paths. The main code which needs to run on your VMS system
whenever it boots is NCD_XDM.EXE, which is NCD's version of xdm, for
managing the logins. We also chose to use TCP/IP through TGV Multinet,
but the DECNET protocol is also available. The simplest way to
accomplish this is to get the VMS version of NCDware from NCD (version
3.1 is recommended). If you don't have an NCD terminal, I'd bet that
NCD_XDM is generic enough to work with your X terminal, as long as it
expects a standard xdm session (I would contact your X terminal vendor).

If you still plan to boot off your UNIX workstations, then all you
need to do is login to your VAX from your UNIX session - usually via
xterm (assuming you have the software to do that - TGV Multinet helps
a lot here, too). You then can set your DECW$DISPLAY variable to your
X terminal and fire up DECterm's or any other X application which you
host on your VMS system (the CREATE/TERMINAL/... command can also be 
used).

