>the PowerPC uses condition flags or exact FP exceptions) of the other
>hot architectures is that it was designed to be one of the best performers
>today, and to scale up in performance well into the future.  The marketing

Of course the marketting guys will say "it is scalable well into the future."
So is/was the Sparc and many others.  While the architecture can make it
*easier*, it doesn't guarantee it, and stupid management/marketting decisions
can easily outweigh its supposed technical merits.  Similarly, a "hard"
to scale processor can be made quite scalable by smart decisions.

>Of course, it's perfectly reasonable to argue that scalability is irrelevant
>if you're deciding what to put on your desktop today or tomorrow, but you
>were making it sound like Alpha was cheating by running at a higher clock
>rate, when the higher clock rate is in many ways a symptom of its high
>performance as much as a cause.

No, it's not cheating.  But it doesn't have some "insight" that the rest
of the uProcessor designers are missing.  They made a different tradeoff
which allowed them to have extremely high clock rates is all, and only a
handful of sales will be because it is "150Mhz" or "200Mhz".  Many other
high performance processors work at much lower frequencies and can achieve
roughly similar results.

