> Okay, who's next?

  Will a Data General and an IBM story do?

  In the mid 70's, SPC Academic Computing was located on the 2nd floor of
McDermott Hall, which was actually 2.5 flights up from the street. Now, when
McD was built, there are conflicting tales of where the elevator went, from
"there never was one planned", to "they couldn't afford it", to "it was stolen
before the job was finished". In any event, no elevator.

  We ordered a 200Mb DG disk drive, and as usual put "Inside delivery required
- no elevator available" on the PO. DG accepted it (suckers). Well, delivery
day came, and the trucker (singular) shows up and says "I got 10 packages, 
total weight 1160 pounds". We figured 1160/10 = 116, no problemo. Oops - it
was more like 1000 + 18*9 pounds. So, we round up a bunch of students and grab
this 1000 pound monstrosity with shockwatches and Tip'n'Tells all over it and
go "One... Two... Three... BA-BOOM!!" up each and every step on the 2.5 flights
of steps.

  We got it to the top and found we couldn't roll it - the casters had devel-
oped major flat spots. We finally dragged it into the computer room (a convert-
ed classroom). The DG repairman came to set it up a few days later, looked at
the shockwatches and Tip'n'Tells and just said "I don't wanna know!". Oddly
enough, it came up and ran fine right away, and had maintained perfect head
alignment.

  Some years later, we moved to our present location at 121 Glenwood and hired
"computer movers" to move all our toys for us. One of the "toys" was an IBM
3138 CPU (a 370/138 to the uninitiated). They decided it was too unwieldy to
put up on the truck, so they'd just roll it. 121 Glenwood is down a nice hill
from McDermott Hall, and they had 5 or 6 guys on the back pushing and 2 guys
on the front steering. They got around the corner (not realizing it was a hill)
and went "Oh, S&*t!!!" and jumped out of the way. The 3138 sailed down the hill
gradually picking up speed, until it came to the bottom, crossed West Side Ave,
and rolled into the cemetary at the foot of the hill, whereupon it hit the curb
and fell over onto the grass.

  The "movers" rescued it and dragged it back up the hill with the moving van
(they put a chain around it and literally dragged it). The brought it into the
new computer room and left it. Our IBM CE came to install it, opened the back
panel where the cables connect, and was amazed to have a clump of grass fall
out... He threw the grass in the garbage and installed the cables and powered
up the system. Nothing happened. [For those who don't know older IBM gear, this
is very unusual - there are check lamps (commonly known as "the red lights")
that indicate what the problem is.

  He went around to the right side of the CPU, where the logic cage is, and
opened up the door and removed the logic cage cover, whereupon he was showered
with humdreds of small logic modules, all of which had become unseated. It took
him about 3 days to put this jigsaw puzzle back together (IBM 370 modules are
only about 2" x 3", and there are *zillions* of them in there, and no two CPUs
are identical, even in the same model). Many years later we saw the Star Trek:
TNG episode where Data gets intoxicated and disassembles the computer and then
has to put it back together. When we saw this, we all went "That's our 3138!".

