In article <2cj6k7$jg4@hsc.usc.edu>, chilling@hsc.usc.edu (John Chillington) writes:
> Using a TU81 tape drive for backups on a VAX 4300 running VMS v5.4-1.  We were
> able to use 3600 ft. magnetic tape for VMS backup without any problems up
> until several weeks ago.  At that point when using a 3600 ft. tape for a full 
> backup started having problems where the backup would abort with fatal error of
> DATALOST.  We have tried using new 3600 ft. tapes but had same problem.  We had
> the TU81 looked at and were told that we should not be using 3600 ft. tape,
> only use 2400 ft. tape.  According to the technician we have been "lucky" that
> the 3600 ft. tapes have worked for over a year.  Trying to confirm if we were
> "lucky" or if there is something wrong with our tape drive. 

  3600' tapes aren't a standard size, but are often found in the seismic and
oil exploration industry (at least until things like 3480, 8mm, and DAT drives
came out). CDC (the makers of the TU80 and TU81 drives) [not Cipher as another
note suggested] don't recommend the use of 3600' tapes. Because the tapes are
thinner, they cause tape head wear at a different angle. Also, many 3600' tapes
are back-coated, which is also unrecommended by CDC because of the crud that
the coating leaves on the drive.

  There were also some driver issues in the past - the driver had a timeout
which was longer than a 2400' tape would take to rewind, but too brief for a
3600' tape. That was changed a few years ago, though, so it shouldn't be your
problem.

  I would suspect that with a good cleaning (especially the tape scraper) and
a new head, the drive would operate with 3600' tapes again. However, I doubt
you're going to be able to talk your service tech into changing the head if it
works with 2400' tapes.

