Indeed, I should have checked references before speaking.  I was
confusing the 11/750 with the 11/780.  According to my 1982-83 VAX
Hardware Handbook, the 11/730 uses something around 7A (bare
processor) to about 12 or 13 (with an RL02 and an R80 in the cabinet)
(those figured are very approximate, as I estimated them from DEC's
figures for RMS current at 90 and 180 Vrms (they don't give a figure
for 110 or 115 or 120, oddly enough) and requires 90-128V 1-phase.
The 11/750 also needs 90-128V 1-phase at 30A or 180-256V 1-phase at
15A (note that they also state that it is capable of drawing a surge
current of around 100A - probably better use slow-blow fuses ;) ).  I
see that as still somewhat of an impediment in the average apartment,
which probably would have no 30A 120V circuits.  The 11/780 requires
3-phase, about 14.4A at 208Vrms.  According to a friend of mine,
however, it was possible to modify the 11/780 for 1-phase, as it was
not actually relying on the phasing to clock anything or drive
multiphase motors.  This wasn't, according to same friend, true of
some of the disk drives and tape drives (TU77 and 78 come to mind, I
think) which did depend on the 3-phase for motor drive.  Anyways, I
digress...
