In article <1993Nov20.040102.26022@news.cs.brandeis.edu>, tober@cs.brandeis.edu (Benjamin Tober) writes:
> The 11/750 also needs 90-128V 1-phase at 30A or 180-256V 1-phase at
> 15A (note that they also state that it is capable of drawing a surge
> current of around 100A - probably better use slow-blow fuses ;) ).  I
> see that as still somewhat of an impediment in the average apartment,
> which probably would have no 30A 120V circuits.

  The 750 has a 30A plug (L5-30) when wired for US operation. It draws a good
deal less than that. DEC has a habit of overkill on the plugs (which can cause
problems - for example, the Arrow/Hart 30A plug has problems getting enough of
a "bite" on #12 wire and can overheat. Personally, I think that's DEC's fault,
not Arrow/Hart's).

> The 11/780 requires
> 3-phase, about 14.4A at 208Vrms.  According to a friend of mine,
> however, it was possible to modify the 11/780 for 1-phase, as it was
> not actually relying on the phasing to clock anything or drive
> multiphase motors.

  The 780 has a 3-phase 30A plug (L6-21). Nothing inside the 780 uses 208V -
look at the power controller and you'll see only regular 110V receptacles.
I've been able to run one on a single 20A circuit for testing. The trick is
to watch the neutral current carefully if you just change the plug.

> This wasn't, according to same friend, true of
> some of the disk drives and tape drives (TU77 and 78 come to mind, I
> think) which did depend on the 3-phase for motor drive.  Anyways, I
> digress...

  TU7x are single-phase 208V units (TU77 is 20A, TU78 is 30A). Some Massbus
disks are single-phase 208V (the RM05, for example), while others (RP06 and
friends) are 3-phase.

  The 86x0 CPU is 3-phase 208V and needs it (the blower has a 3-phase motor).
I've checked the current draw of a loaded 8650 and found it draws about 12/
12/14 amps.

