208V, three-phase coming in, but going out of the distribution box
(whatever it is called) is 120V single-phase. I have pretty much confirmed
this with a few people. The HSC50 is 208V, three-phase, but inside it 
is nothing but 120V (this I have verified). I havn't seen the insides of
a 6000 yet, so that has not been verified.

It does make sense, of course, to have 208V, three-phase for 1.5 
reasons:
1) All the existing iron that the VAX 6000 replaces is 208V three-phase.
Therefore, it makes no sense to have the VAX 6000 use a 120V 30A recepticle
when most big-iron VAX shops don't have 120V available. 

.5) If it didn't require 208V, three-phase, it wouldn't be "big-iron"
anymore, would it? ;-)

>  It's rated to have a max draw of 8
>amps (208V RMS), ~1600 Watts.  So it's a hair dryer.

Are you sure about that? (Not the hair dryer part, the wattage part. ;-)

Having multiple phases throws a monkey wrench into the IV=Watts 
equation. Whatever the case, it appears that it will cost about 
$2k-2.5k/year to feed a VAX 6000 on residential power. Then there's
the HSC50 and SC008 and whatever 9-track tape drive... (Hey, if you
didn't want VAXcluster hardware, then it wouldn't be worth bothering
with the big iron in the first place. ;-)

