In article <1993Nov21.181006.3351@adobe.com> kolling@adobe.com (Karen Kolling) writes:
=>
=>What is Linux, anyway?  I'd like a not-too-quasi UNIX to run on my
=>home system to be, and I'm not thrilled about the possibility of ponying up
=>$1K for an OSF license.  Can I run X/Motif with Linux?
=>
=>signed,
=>
=>still trying to decide between an Alpha AXP 150 and an XL 486

   Linux and {386,Free,Net}BSD are freely available Unix clones.
Linux is built from scratch, while 386BSD (and its derivitives,
FreeBSD and NetBSD) is built around the Net/2 release from UC
Berkeley. All versions run XFree86, a set of X11R5 servers for SVGA
and various accelerated video cards. I've heard rumors of Motif
being available in binary form, but I haven't seen anything.
   I was thinking of getting an Alpha PC AXP, but OSF/1 wasn't
available, and I really didn't want NT on my machine. So now I'm
writing this from a DECpc 466d2 MTE with an S3-928 card running
NetBSD and XFree86. NetBSD is designed to be portable, and there
are rumors of an Alpha port, so I figured I could always get an
Alpha later and network them together.
   One thing you would give up is the ability to run commercial
software. I've heard rumors of a few commercial apps for Linux,
but I've not followed Linux as much as *BSD. Most free software
compiles out-of-the-box on *BSD, and (as I understand it) with
some minor tweaks on Linux.

