> >Could anybody tell me if my DECpc 433 is compatible with Linux ? The specs
> >are as follows
> >
> >  Intel 486/33

I dont know the machine in question, but if it's an IBM PC Compatable,
it will be. The only fancy bit to ensure is that your graphics card is
supported by Xfree86, the X Server that comes with Linux, if you want
to run X-Windows as well.

> What is Linux, anyway?  I'd like a not-too-quasi UNIX to run on my
> home system to be, and I'm not thrilled about the possibility of ponying up
> $1K for an OSF license.  Can I run X/Motif with Linux?

Linux is a Unix clone, written by Linus Torvalds helped by a cast of
thousands worldwide. I can only point you to the FAQ in
comp.os.linux.help - I know its about 1500 articles long, but the FAQ
is worth it!

Linux is, of course, completely free, and downloadable direct from many
FTP sites worldwide, including Finland, USA, UK, (and other places I've
forgotten now).

There is a myth going round that Linux is not stable - it *is* stable!
The 'current' release (ie not the forefront alpha release for bug
hunters) is 0.99 patch level 13, and this is very usable. Some people
run their Linux boxes as mini-computers, with 10-15 users logged on, no
problems.

Yes, you can run Motif with Linux, but you'll have to _buy_ Motif & its
libraries, since Motif is a commercial product. Open Look comes free
with the SLS distribution of Linux, and other free window managers are
available.

Enjoy!


