In article <CGEJ9r.I2o@dvorak.amd.com> jerry@diablo.amd.com (Jerry Williamson) writes:

   In article <1993Nov12.192353.3758@peavax.mlo.dec.com> ervin@pinbot.enet.dec.com (Joseph James Ervin) writes:
   >
   >I keep seeing the word "superscalar" (sp?) in reference

   spelling is correct!

   >to current high peformance CPU designs.
   >
   >What does this mean?
   >
   >Does it refer to micro- or macro- pipelining?
   >Does it refer to multiple-issue?

   My interpretation of the concept of what is meant by
   a superscalar architecture is relatively simple.

   To me superscalar means an architecture that through
   the use of parallel pipelining and other features 
   like branch prediction, etc. (can you say Pentium?)
   you will have more than one path of execution per
   instruction and thus have the ability of executing
   multiple instructions in one clock cycle. 

Although most superscalar processors use pipelined execution and branch
prediction, these are completely orthogonal issues.  You certainly could
build a superscalar part that had *no* pipelining and used no branch
prediction, though it probably wouldn't be all that interesting.  Similarly,
you can build a single issue machine that is deeply pipelined and uses
branch prediction.

bill
