>>In article <CGJJ5A.6Dt@uceng.uc.edu>,
>>Ross Bagley <rabagley@deerfly.occ.uc.edu> wrote:
[...]
>>>so the 386 the 486 and the pentium are all superscalar...
>>[...] The
>>386 and the 486 have never been superscalar chips.
 
> What's the technical term that describes the fact that a SUB (AL,imm8) 
> operation takes:
>                           Clocks
>                   486    386  286    86
>                    1      2    3     4

Faster   :-) :-) (sorry, I couldn't resist) 

> Is that pipelining.  Arg.  I'm confused.

As far as I know, yes, pipelining is what helps this to be done, the
maths unit can "look ahead" in the pipeline and get everything it can
sorted out in advance of having to actually do the instruction,
instead of seeing SUB etc, then saying ooh, i'd better go fetch some 
operands, which takes time, they are already sitting in a known place
somewhere internal to the chip and it can just go ahead and use them.


Superscalar means that the _effective_ execution time for an
instruction is less than 1 clock, ie (making up some random numbers)
200 instructions completed in about 100 clocks (Pentium ish) or 300
instructions in about 100 clocks (PPC601 ish) assuming an ideal
instruction stream for both.

PS:  I'm not an expert; the above is just how I believe things are.

Cheers
	Ken.
