>In article <1993Nov12.192353.3758@peavax.mlo.dec.com> ervin@pinbot.enet.dec.com (Joseph James Ervin) writes:
> >I keep seeing the word "superscalar" (sp?) in reference

>Also, means that more than one instruction can be executed per CPU clock cycle.
>Pentium can, for one.

It's better to say "capable to execute more than 1 instruction per clock
cycle without explicit relation between instructions". I don't mean
instruction interdependances. One may imagine an architecture which
allows compiler to shedule bunch of instructions for clock cycles
explicitly. This approach allows to lunch up to 8 instructions per
clock. Conventional superscalar design doesn't exploit such level
of parallelism.

