In article <CGMu3p.1vrp@park.se>, 
p5ta26@park.se (John Vestberg) writes:

> Is it possible to use a SCSI-II controller with the MicroVAX II?

Absolutely!

> Where can I get the controller? 

I have a very nice Q-Bus SCSI controller from Andromeda Systems, 
called the SCDC.   It handles the usual seven SCSI devices (both 
tape and disk), plus two floppy disks (inexpensive PC style, 
not the expensive SCSI ones).   All the disks appear as "DU" type 
devices and all the tapes as "MU" devices.   (Note: VMS only 
supports a single tape drive on a MU tape controller.)

The controller also has 2MB of cache which really helps
with slow devices like CDROMs.

A really neat feature is the ability to partition a single
physical disk drive into multiple DU units and control
which ones are cached.   I have a system with two 2GB
drives split into a total of eight 500MB logical units.

This controller isn't cheap but it is really good.   I had
an application that took eight hours using an Emulex QD-34
and a pair of Fujitsu 2333 drives.   Switching to the Andromeda
SCDC and a pair of Seagate ST42400 drives cut over two hours
from the run time.   Now it is almost totally CPU bound.
