In article <1993Nov18.093051.743@acs.eku.edu>, acssysdsc@acs.eku.edu writes:
> How do you know if you need to change the cluster size on an RA90?
> 
> Our current cluster size is 3. The disk is about 80% full, externally
> fragments quickly (it's the newsreader disk), and has about 25% internal
> file fragmentation. The only tool I have at present is DECps Adviser.
> I was thinkin of changing the cluster size to 2.

In general, decreasing the cluster size will increase fragmentation (there
are more possible clusters per file which may not be contiguous).  Also,
the minimum cluster size for an RA90 is 3.  The minimum cluster size is
calculated:

                  disk_size_in_blocks/(255*4096)

For an RA90, this comes out to:

                  2376153/1044480 = 2.27496

                  round up to next integer = 3

> Any suggestions/recommendations?

If you wanted to decrease fragmentation, you could increase the cluster
size to 4; depending on the news item sizes, you may not lose much disk
space this way (your bitmap will be smaller, which will reclaim some of
the room).  Since you're already at 80%, you might do it on a trial
basis.  Don't forget to pre-allocate file headers on the item disk!

> DEC DFO is ordered, but I question if it will be able to keep this disk 
> defragmented? Anyone else have experience with this?

I don't know of a defragmenter that could; news here is constantly adding
during the day with a few deletions, then lots of them at expire time.
The disk and file system I/O is already large, and a defragger would have
to work its way in somewhere.  We're the only feed for two other sites,
so we have enough trouble keeping up as it is.
