In article <2bufsk$ehd@lll-winken.llnl.gov>, oberman@ptavv.llnl.gov writes:
|> In Article <1993Nov11.204536.29025@news.arc.nasa.gov>, I wrote:
|> [...]
|> >possible.  In the meantime, however, DECnet performance takes a much bigger
|> >hit than TCP performance, since TCP's retransmission algorithm works well, 
|> >and DECnet (Phase IV) doesn't.
|> 
|> The algorithm works quite well. It's the implementation detail that messed
|> things up. Sadly it's simply not worth fixing in a protocol with a 16 bit
|> address field. (I think that is the only "real" problem.)

I'll grant that the algorithm works, but I still wouldn't say that it works 
that well.  The combination of:  a) no information about variance in the 
RTT's, just a running average,  b) the addition of timed-out RTT values 
feeding back into the DELAY calculation, and  c) the use of integer DELAY
(min=1 sec) in the retransmission algorithm,  makes for some very unfortunate
problem cases.

It's quite distressing seeing a LAN environment where two LANs are separated
with a router (or bridge) which is dropping a few too many packets,  and
the DECnet session first sees a couple 5 sec hangs, then a 10 sec, 15 sec,
etc., as each of the hangs adds back into the DELAY calculation, and DELAY
starts incrementing 1, 2, 3, etc.

This has gotten rather far off field for "comp.dcom.sys.cisco", I'm afraid,
and I'm not following comp.sys.dec currently, so I'll wrap it up.

Warren Van Camp
NASA Science Internet


