In article <1993Nov12.011313.5122@news.arc.nasa.gov>, vancamp@uchini.arc.nasa.gov (Warren Van Camp) writes:
|>
|>I'll grant that the algorithm works, but I still wouldn't say that it works 
|>that well.  The combination of:  a) no information about variance in the 
|>RTT's, just a running average,  b) the addition of timed-out RTT values 
|>feeding back into the DELAY calculation, and  c) the use of integer DELAY
|>(min=1 sec) in the retransmission algorithm,  makes for some very unfortunate
|>problem cases.

The preceding paragragh is definitely not true for the NSP implementation
I own and maintain.  If my NSP detects a round trip time of a packets that's
seriously out of whack, it will suppress delayed acks (ie. increase the number
of samples) until it becomes stable again.  Timed-out packets are explicitly
not used in calculating the RTT.  Internally the RTT is kept in milliseconds.
But for a packet, the RTT is not allowed to drop below one second.

It's a trade off between having a multi-second network glitch (routing
reconfiguration for instance) and having lost packets.  One of tenets used
when NSP was designed was that lost packets are infrequent.  The P-to-P
datalinks that DECnet uses (primarily DDCMP and HDLC handle retransmission
of damaged or missing frame so that shouldn't be a problem.  On LANs, CRC
errors should very infrequest and unless you get excessive collisions,
packets on a LAN should not disappear.  If they do you have a configuration
or equipment problem.

Another thing a NSP implementation can do is recognize that a packet has been
lost and pretend a timeout has happened.  But assuming that your network works
properly, the most frequent reason of lost packets is that you overrun the
receiver.

For this you want to drop your pipeline quota such you won't have as many
packets outstanding and so there is less chance of overruning the buffering
at the NSP layer (this is primarily a problem with the VMS Phase IV NSP
implementation).  Also, you may want to increase the receive buffers on the
DECnet lines.  If you see user buffer unavailables on the circuit, then start
increasing the line receive buffers until they go away (if possible).

|>It's quite distressing seeing a LAN environment where two LANs are separated
|>with a router (or bridge) which is dropping a few too many packets,  and
|>the DECnet session first sees a couple 5 sec hangs, then a 10 sec, 15 sec,
|>etc., as each of the hangs adds back into the DELAY calculation, and DELAY
|>starts incrementing 1, 2, 3, etc.

What's a few too packets? 50% 10% 1%?  If you have a router/bridge that drops
packets even when the path between the two nodes is not saturated, then you've
bought a piece of equipment that violates the assumptions made when DECnet
was architected.  Of course DECnet will not behave well with that
router/bridge.


