In article <13NOV199313283561@utkvx.utk.edu> dellwo@utkvx.utk.edu (This Side Up) writes:
>When the processor reads an instruction from memory can't it
>read only one instruction per clock cycle? How does a
>superscalar processor execute more than one command per
>cycle. Does the compiler combine commands into one long
>word.

  Newer microprocessors typically have a 64-bit or wider path to
memory.  On an x86, this means you could conceivably load up to eight
8-bit instructions in one clock cycle (though most instructions are
more than one byte); on a 601, two 32-bit instructions; etc.

  Just as importantly, once instructions are in the cache, the
internal data path from cache to instruction buffer can be large.
It's 128 bits in the PPC 601, which means up to eight instructions can
be loaded from cache in one clock cycle.  I'm not familiar with the
Pentium or AXP processors' internal buses, but I suspect they're at
least as large.

  To execute multiple instructions, the processor looks simultaneously
at more than one instruction.  On the PPC 601, there are three units
which examine the instruction buffer.  One can start an integer op,
one can start a floating-point op, one can decode branches.  There's
some logic which guarantees that instructions which should not be
executed don't get "committed" (have results written back) -- e.g. an
integer operation that occurs after a branch, when the processor
thought the branch would go one way and then it turned out not to.

  Again, I'm not familiar with the Pentium, but I believe it has two
integer pipelines which would mean it could execute two integer
instructions at once if they don't depend on each other.  Not sure
about the FP logic.

  Really high-end superscalar machines, like the VAX 9000 series, can
decode complex instructions into simpler data-flow units and use a lot
of special hardware to execute pieces of the instructions out of
order, too, but I don't think any microprocessors do yet.

>By the way, I've always called 32 bits a "long word."
>What do you call 64 bits now?  Just curious.

  Depends where you come from.  On most Crays, 64 bits is a word.  I've
heard "doubleword" used for 64-bit stuff in VAX and 680x0 environments.
Maybe x86 people use the same?
--
Anton Rang (rang@gnu.ai.mit.edu)


