(included text follows)

WINDOWS NT KERNEL-MODE DEVICE DRIVERS FOR OpenVMS DRIVER WRITERS

This seminar will provide detailed information on kernel-mode
device drivers in Windows NT, with emphasis on "porting" the
attendee's knowledge and experience in writing OpenVMS device
drivers to the Windows NT environment.  The I/O subsystems, and
in particular the architecture of device drivers, of Windows NT
and OpenVMS are very similar in many respects.  By briefly
describing the similarities and concentrating on the differences,
we expect to be able to cover a lot of ground quickly.

Topics to be covered include:

      o  Windows NT concepts for driver writers
      o  The Windows NT input/output API
      o  A brief "walkthrough" of an I/O request
      o  Dispatch routines
      o  Buffered, direct, and "neither" I/O
      o  The StartIO routine
      o  Hardware considerations
      o  Interrupt service and DPC routines
      o  IoCompletion routines
      o  Other driver routines
      o  Layered device drivers
      o  Handling multiple I/O requests
      o  Loading and testing a driver
      o  Debugging techniques

Note that user-mode device drivers (chiefly display and printer
formatters; I hate calling them "drivers") will not be covered,
                                                ---
except for a brief description.

Speaker:

Jamie Hanrahan is an independent consultant, specializing in
application design, internals, device drivers, and network
programming under VMS.  He has over ten years of experience in
VMS applications, internals, and device drivers, both through
teaching and in actual software engineering environments, and is
now expanding his focus to include Windows NT.  He is the
co-author (with Lee Leahy, of Digital Equipment Corporation) of
the book Advanced VMS Device Driver Techniques, and is writing a
         -------- --- ------ ------ ----------
book on Windows NT device drivers, to be published in the Spring
of 1994 by O'Reilly and Associates.  He is also known as one of
the principal authors of "DECUS uucp", a dial-up mail and file
transfer utility for VMS.

Prerequisites:

Ideally, those attending this seminar should have attended
Digital's course on VMS drivers, or have equivalent experience,
and should have written at least one VMS device driver; or should
have similar experience under Windows NT.  Device driver
experience on other operating systems, particularly MS-DOS, Unix,
and OS/2, is not as directly transferrable to Windows NT, but
will still be helpful.

(end)



