In article <RANG.93Nov13172443@kropotkin.gnu.ai.mit.edu>,
Anton Rang <rang@gnu.ai.mit.edu> wrote:
>In article <13NOV199313283561@utkvx.utk.edu> dellwo@utkvx.utk.edu 
>(This Side Up) writes:
>>When the processor reads an instruction from memory can't it
>>read only one instruction per clock cycle? How does a
>>superscalar processor execute more than one command per
>>cycle. Does the compiler combine commands into one long
>>word.
>

	But nothing is stopping you from reading many instructions
and store them into a buffer, then send more then one instruction
out at a time though.

>
>  To execute multiple instructions, the processor looks simultaneously
>at more than one instruction.  On the PPC 601, there are three units
>which examine the instruction buffer.  One can start an integer op,
>one can start a floating-point op, one can decode branches.  There's
>some logic which guarantees that instructions which should not be
>executed don't get "committed" (have results written back) -- e.g. an
>integer operation that occurs after a branch, when the processor
>thought the branch would go one way and then it turned out not to.

	To clarify my previous point.  The instruction is read
one at a time into a buffer, but each instructions goes into the
branch unit first to do some branch predict processing, then none
branch instructions are issued into the integer unit in parallel
with float point unit.  

	Right there, the ability to issue more than one instruction
per clock is a "broad" definition of what superscalar is.  At least
this is the definition according to one of the father's of 
pipeline architecture Dr. E.S. Davidson. who is one of my profs 
right now.

- Chung Yang
 
