SUBJECT:  CARRERA TO BUILD LOW-END, HIGH-POWER COMPUTERS WITH DIGITAL'S NEW
          ALPHA CHIPS
SOURCE:   Business Wire via First! by INDIVIDUAL, Inc.
DATE:     November 10, 1993
INDEX:    [1]
- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

  LAGUNA HILLS, Calif.--(BUSINESS WIRE) via First! -- Carrera Computers Inc.
Wednesday announced it will soon begin shipping complete personal computer
workstations using the latest Alpha AXP 21064 and 21066 microprocessors from
Digital Equipment Corp.

  The Laguna Hills company, which sells motherboards and subsystems built
around the Reduced Instruction Set Computer (RISC) technology, has
established a systems division to manufacture and market its new Pantera
Series I and II personal workstations, powered by Digital's Alpha AXP 166
megahertz and AXP 200MHz CPUs.

  "We are very impressed with the design of Digital's new Alpha chips," said
Rod Frye, Carrera vice chairman.  "In introducing the 21066, Digital has
created the first microprocessor with an integrated PCI (Peripheral
Component Interconnect) bus architecture.  That, along with the
affordability, enables PC vendors to offer very high performance systems at
much lower prices than existing workstation-level products," he said.

  The company also will offer the new workstations in motherboard form to
original equipment manufacturers through its board level division, which
also provides building-block CPUs based on Digital's Alpha 150, and MIPS
R4000 and R4400 processors.

  "We believe the emerging Windows NT market will require the highest level
of performance today's technology can offer," said Bruce Faust, Carrera co-
founder.  "The new Alpha chips make it possible, for the first time, to
raise NT performance to the next level while providing systems at the same
price as Intel-based PCs are sold today."

  Faust said the 200MHz Alpha 21064 is the first system to surpass the 100
mark in two performance testing categories (SPEC int 108 and SPEC fp 169).
The Alpha 21066 motherboard, tested at three times faster than the most
powerful Intel-powered system (SPEC int 70 and SPEC fp 106).

  They also will feature dark blue and granite colors unless customers
request the standard off-white for the case and optional 14- or 17-inch
monitors.  The Windows NT version 3.1 operating system is included with both
Pantera models.

  "We believe these systems, at these prices and with these features, and
packaged in a sexy case, could be the first RISC PCs to penetrate the home
office market," said Mark Perry, director of sales and marketing.

  Perry said the Pantera I will carry a $3,995 price tag and will house the
Alpha 21066 166MHz "baby AT-sized" motherboard in a standard desktop case.
It will feature 16MB of memory, 128 kilobytes of secondary cache memory, a
250MB SCSI hard disk drive, 3.5-inch 1.44MB floppy disk drive, 230-watt
power supply and a TGA accelerated video adapter.

  Pantera I includes 64-bit memory and processor buses and a 32-bit I/O bus,
two 32-bit PCI slots and three 16-bit ISA slots.  Controllers are embedded
in the motherboard for SCSI-2 at 10 MB/sec. throughput and Ethernet at
10Mbits/sec., two asynchronous RS-232 serial ports, a Centronics parallel
port, keyboard and mouse ports, and an IBM-compatible floppy disk drive
controller.

  The Pantera II will sell for $6,995.  The system, based on the 21064 64-
bit RISC CPU and running at 200MHz clock speed, will be encased in a tower
chassis with a 250-watt power supply and feature: 32MB of main system memory
and 1MB of secondary cache, SCSI CD-ROM drive and a 500MB SCSI hard disk
drive, a TGA accelerated video adapter and 3.5-inch 1.44MB floppy disk
drive.

  Pantera II's configuration also includes a 128-bit intervleaved memory
bus, 64-bit processor bus and 32-bit I/O bus, two 32-bit PCI slots and three
16-bit ISA slots.  Embedded on the motherboard are controllers for an SCSI-2
PCI at 10MB/sec., Ethernet PC at 10Mbits/sec., two asynchronous serial ports
and a Centronics parallel port, keyboard and mouse ports, and IBM-compatible
floppy disk drive.

  The systems will be manufactured in the United States and will be sold
initially by Carrera's telemarketing sales force and directly via an 800
number, 576-RISC, Frye said.

  Carrera Computers has headquarters at 23181 Verdugo Drive, Suite 105A,
Laguna Hills, Calif. 92653.  Telephone:  714/707-5051.  Fax: 714/707-5053.

