In article <2btvn2$qdp@lll-winken.llnl.gov> oberman@ptavv.llnl.gov writes:
>In Article <CGBno5.B4r@wm.estec.esa.nl>
>dstaffor@estec.esa.nl (Dave Stafford x4437) writes:
>>Well, thank you those who replied to the DECnet performance questions,
>>both those diehards that I upset, and to those who agreed. 
>>
>>There were a few useful suggestions, but the bottom line is still that
>>DECnet does not perform over a routed or bridged network if packets are
>>dropped. (And I do know that bridges operate at the data link layer, and
>>I also know they affect DECnet when they drop packets.)

>Mr. Stafford talks about bridges dropping packets. This indicates something is
>broken, and I don't mean the protocol. Bridges should NOT drop packets.

In a pure DECnet network (i.e. one where bridges can set the congestion
indication bit) this is true.  But try using DECnet over bridges that don't
know anything about the congestion indication bit and you could be in a lot of
trouble.  I believe the problem is that DECnet is not designed to cope with
packet losses terribly well (unlike TCP) because it was assumed that congestion
was going to be controlled with Raj Jain's scheme that relies on the congestion
indication bit being set when there is congestion.

If you want to see how TCP copes with losses caused by congestion in bridges or
anywhere else then read "Some Observations on the Dynamics of a Congestion
Control Algorithm" by Shenker, Zhang and Clark, Computer Communication Review,
October 1990.  You will see that packet loss is a normal and
not-terribly-problematical part of life in TCP networks.

Chris O'Neill
Telecom Australia Research Labs


