>"QUADWORD?"

Our convention in the 29K arena is to refer to 32-bit data as 'words'.

When we are using 64-bit literals (double precision constants) I offhandedly
refer to them as 'doublewords'. 

Eight bits is and always will be a byte.

Sixteen bits used to be a word until 32-bit architecture came along --
which temporarily changed the convention. Thus 16-bits were referred 
to as "short words" and 32-bits were known as "long words"

Now with 32-bit architecture now the mainstream, it is now

32   word

16   half-word
 
8    byte

4    nibble.

I assume when 64-bit architectures take over in the next 5 years it
will look something like ----

128  double-word

64   word

32   half-word

16   quarter-word

8    byte

4    nibble

2    cracker

1    crumb   (what else would you call 1 bit?)


Cheers,

