In article <13NOV199313283561@utkvx.utk.edu>, dellwo@utkvx.utk.edu (This Side Up) writes:
> When the processor reads an instruction from memory can't it
> read only one instruction per clock cycle. How does a
> superscalar processor execute more than one command per
> cycle. Does the compiler combine commands into one long
> word.

   This is where the on-chip cache's really come into play.  Although the
path from external memory to the chip is generally only one instruction wide,
the path from the internal cache's to the core can be considerably wider.
This of course means that on the first pass through any section of code you
are only going to be able to execute one instruction per cycle.  However, on
subsequent passes (as long as the routine remains cached), you can execute as
many instructions per cycle as your hardware and code will allow.
> 
> By the way, I've always called 32 bits a "long word."
> What do you call 64 bits now?  Just curious.

   Actually, your terminology is based on the assumption that a 16 bit variable
is a word.  Usage here in i960 land is that a word is whatever the natural size
of the ability of your processor to handle is.  In the case of a 32-bit
processor, a word would be a 32-bit value.  Therefore, a 64-bit value would be
a long word, and a 128 bit value would be a quad word.  And to answer the
question in advance, a 16-bit value would be a short word.

   It is my understanding that this corresponds fairly well to the way some
compiled languages are spec'ed.  Somebody will surely correct me if I'm wrong,
but isn't an integer in C defined to be a MINIMUM of 16 bits, but otherwise the
natural register size.  I think that it is completely legal (according to the
C spec) for an integer to be a 32-bit value on a 32-bit machine.  I seem to
recall being bitten by this when doing some programming a couple of years ago.
A program using a 32 value assigned to an integer worked when compiled on one
work station and didn't when compiled on another.  Defining the variable as long
int solved the problem.

TJ
