In article <PLATYPUS.93Nov13221520@nimbus.som.cwru.edu>,
 platypus@nimbus.som.cwru.edu (Gary Kacmarcik) writes:
> please note the newsgroups that you are posting to, and be aware that
> stating definitively that 64-bit quantities are called "quadword"s is
> misleading _even_ if you're posting only to comp.sys.dec.  i highly
> doubt that _every_ DEC system released has a 16-bit word so that
> 64-bits would make four of them.
> 
> word sizes (and multiple of word sizes) are entirely system dependent.

True.  The word size on a VAX is 32 bits, but it's called a LONGWORD there.
Since "WORD" was used for years to mean 16 bits, a new term was invented to
distinguish between them.  So, the word size and the size of "word" became
two different things there.  So, on a VAX with a 32 bit word, a word is
still 16 bits.  It's just the terminology.

>>    I cannot give an authoritative history, but recall that PDP-11 (and
>>    other PDP's?) had BYTEs and WORDs (8 and 16 bits respectively).  When
>>    the VAX architecture came along, 32 bit entities got named LONGWORDs
>>    for the obvious reason.  However, even the VAX architecture uses 64
>>    bit entities, for example, the "system time QUADWORD", and there are
>>    Macro instructions for dealing with them, e.g. MOVQ does a quadword
>>    move.
> 
> again, this only means that a 64-bit quantity is called a quadword on a
> VAX.  on the PowerPC (and on most of the new processors where 32-bits is
> a word), a 64-bit quantity is called a doubleword.

Again, this is just terminology, since the VAX is also a 32-bit chip.

>>    I haven't seen any documentation for Alpha AXP's so I don't know
>>    what these things will be called in that architecture...
> 
> since the Alpha doesn't consider 16-bits to be a word, you can almost be
> guaranteed that is not called "quadword".

Well, don't be so sure.  Just because the AXP has a 64-bit word, my guess
is that at least the VMS name for the 64-bit data type will still be a
quadword, not a word.  Even though VMS never shipped on a 16-bit system,
it keeps the 16-bit terms.  I don't know what the 64-bit data type may be
called on other OS's running on AXP.  And, on other hardware platforms,
they can call them what they like.

"Word" terminology just isn't good for describing bit sizes.  If you keep
them constant, you have confusion between the data type and the architecture.
If you make them consistent with the hardware word size, you don't know how
many bits it is unless you know the platform.

How about "octabyte"?
