tjehl@sedona.intel.com (Timothy Jehl) writes:
] In article <>, rabagley@deerfly.occ.uc.edu (Ross Bagley) writes:
] >
] > Actually any processor with a pipelined architecture is "superscalar" due
] > to the fact that it is working on several instructions at once.
] > 
] > The 80386 is actually considered the most successful superscalar CISC 
] > processor to date.  Just so happens that most modern superscalar designs
] > use multiple pipelines as well...
] > 
] > so the 386 the 486 and the pentium are all superscalar...
]
]    Proof positive that a little education can be dangerous.

Indeed. Ross Bagley's definition of "superscalar" is totally wrong.

]    Common use of the term superscaler implies that you can, on a regular
] basis, retire more than one instruction per cycle. 

I think the definition of "superscalar" actually depends on being able
to _issue_, not retire, more than one instruction per clock. This 
differentiates an Intel 80486 (which _could_, I suppose, complete an 
integer and an FP operation in the same cycle, but can't _issue_ them 
in the same cycle) from a Intel 80960CA, which can, for example, _issue_
(and retire, for that matter) an add, a load and a branch all in one cycle.

] The Pentium is only the second superscaler
] processor put out by Intel, and the first was not an x86.

More like the third, fourth, fifth or sixth, depending on how
and what you count. The Intel i960(R) processor family has three
or four superscalar members : the CA, CF, and (counting as either
one or two) the MM and MX. The Intel i860 processors MIGHT be
considered superscalar, or might be considered VLIW, depending.
