"Common use" is the key phrase here.  I recall some DEC-internal discussion
of the issue where the formal definition of superscalar was entered.  It
contained words to the effect that a superscalar architecture of order N
can issue N instructions per clock cycle (I'm using fuzzy wording here,
but the key point is that there are orders of "superscalarness.")  Anyway,
there was no lower limit on order, so any CPU capable of issuing one
instruction per clock cycle can be considered to be a superscalar processor
of order 1.

"Superscalar" is to CPUs as "Superheterodyne" is to radar detectors.  It's a
marketing term as much as anything...  To be fair, "common" usage among
CPU designers has "superscalar" meaning "superscalar of order >1", but
marketeers have never shown great respect for common technical usage,
especially if the formal definition allows them to say a nifty new buzzword
without even lying. :-)

