No, that's not at all correct. "Scalar" processors issue no more than 1
instruction per cycle; "supercalar" processors issue MORE than that,
that's why the "super" prefix is there (i.e. "above scalar"). That's
not "common useage", that's the _definition_. Yes the term "order(n)
superscalar" is used, but just as an "Order 0" polynomial isn't
a polynomial at all (it's a constant), "Order 1 superscalar" processsors
are not superscalar. That all processors have an "order" of
"superscalarness" does NOT make them all superscalar.

I will NOT have a PERFECTLY GOOD engineering term diluted into
meaninglessness by know-nothings know-it-alls.

