The following is a white paper developed jointly by Digital's OpenVMS and 
DECnet/OSI product groups.

I post it here because there seems to be a lot of apprehension about upgrading
nodes to DECnet/OSI. I hope that this paper will help to alleviate some of
that apprehension.

Dave Garrod
Networks Engineering
Digital Equipment Corporation


Title:  OpenVMS VAX Version 6.0 and the move to DECnet/OSI V5.7
------

Overview: 
--------

This article has been created to help customers who are interested in
taking advantage of the new functionality introduced in OpenVMS VAX
Version 6.0 and who require X.25 support. X.25 support on OpenVMS VAX
V6.0 is available only with DECnet/OSI (Digital Network Architecture 
Phase V). Other OSI product functions (such as OSAK and  VOTS) have 
been fully incorporated into DECnet/OSI and are no longer available 
as standalone products. DECnet/OSI is now a prerequisite for layered 
products dependent on Wide Area Network Device Drivers (WANDD). 

Moving to DECnet/OSI is generally a matter of upgrading the individual
DECnet nodes. To take full advantage of the new features of DECnet/OSI 
(described below) elements of the network backbone may also require 
upgrades. Solid up front planning for the network as a whole will
dramatically simplify the system managers job in this transition. 
This article explains the steps involved in upgrading the nodes and 
moving the network to a DECnet/OSI environment. The differences
between DECnet-VAX (Phase IV) and the DECnet/OSI environment are 
described in order to help customers understand how to effectively 
transition from DECnet-VAX to DECnet/OSI.  A Question & Answer 
section is included to address specific questions. 

OpenVMS VAX Version 6.0
-----------------------

OpenVMS VAX Version 6.0 was available worldwide in July, 1993. 
Version 6.0 contains a great deal of new functionality such as security 
enhancements that have allowed OpenVMS VAX Version 6.0 to gain a C2 security 
rating from the National Computer Security Center (NCSC), extended physical 
addressing, extended virtual addressing, adaptive pool management, a 
virtual I\O cache, dynamic load balancing for MSCP (Mass Storage Control 
Protocol) devices, ISO 9660 support, and a host of other enhancements 
(refer to the OpenVMS VAX Version 6.0 New Features Manual). 

Implementing this new functionality in OpenVMS VAX Version 6.0 has had
some affect on how Digital layered products work with this version of
the operating system.  Most of the Digital's layered products are
already available for customers who are ready to take advantage of
OpenVMS VAX Version 6.0; however, a few products are not yet available
(refer to the OpenVMS VAX Version 6.0 Release Notes Addendum for a
complete list of products and availability dates). 

One of the products yet to be released is DECnet/OSI V5.7 for OpenVMS 
VAX. This release will provide DECnet/OSI services, including X.25 and 
Wide Area Network support, for both the current release, OpenVMS VAX V6.0, 
and the upcoming release, OpenVMS VAX V6.1. Users of OpenVMS VAX V6.0 who 
require OSI services will need to upgrade from DECnet-VAX to DECnet/OSI 
V5.7 when it is released in Q1 (calendar year) 1994. 

Moving to DECnet/OSI:
--------------------

A number of automated tools (DECnet Migration utilities and the NCP
Emulator) and simplified configuration procedures are available to
help ease the migration from DECnet-VAX. Those who have already made
the transition have found that configuring DECnet/OSI was less 
complicated than originally anticipated. 

The complexity of the transition from DECnet-VAX to DECnet/OSI varies
depending on the complexity of the existing network. The smaller the
network, the easier the transition. For larger networks, careful
planning is required. In either case, the transition can be
accomplished over time - without disrupting day to day operations. 

DECnet-VAX and DECnet/OSI: What's new?
--------------------------------------

In order for network managers to plan the network transition, it is
first necessary to understand the differences between DECnet-VAX and
DECnet/OSI. DECnet/OSI is the implementation of the fifth Phase of the
Digital Network Architecture (DNA). DNA Phase V integrates the Open
System Interconnect (OSI) protocols with DECnet protocols to create a
network protocol stack that simultaneously supports both existing
DECnet applications and the international standard OSI applications.
Using DECnet/OSI, applications can connect and communicate with peer
applications on any OSI based system, whether from Digital or from
other vendors. The addition of the OSI protocols is the primary
difference between DECnet-VAX and DECnet/OSI. 


DECnet                         OSI
Applications                   Applications 
      \                        /
       \  (Standard           / (Standard
        \  DECnet            /   OSI 
         \ Interfaces)      /    Interfaces)
	+------+       +------+
	|      |       |      | 
	|  DNA |       | OSI  |
	|      |       |      | 
	|      |       |      | 
	|      |       |      | 
	+---------------------+   
	|   Transport         | 
	+---------------------+   (Integrated
	|    Network          |    Lower 
	+---------------------+    Layers)
	|    Data Link        |
	+---------------------+
	|    Physical         |
	+---------------------+


Figure 1 - DECnet/OSI
---------------------------------------------------------

There are other differences. DECnet/OSI contains many new 
features designed to enhance networking capabilities. These new 
features include:

o Global Naming/Directory services

In large networks, the number of nodes and users makes it extremely 
difficult to manage any form of local directory service - end systems 
simply cannot handle all the directory files and perform designated 
computing functions. Global (or distributed) naming services allow 
large networks to easily store, manage, and access addressing 
information for (potentially) millions of network objects, such as end 
systems, users, printers, files, and disks. With the explosion of PCs 
as the desktop device of choice, millions of nodes in a network is 
becoming a reality.

o Optional Local Naming/Directory services

Smaller networks do not have as critical a need for global directory 
services. Networks having 150 or fewer nodes can still maintain local 
directory services.

o Expanded Network management capabilities via the new Network Command 
Language (NCL)

As networks become more complex, the management of networks has to 
provide more as well. NCL allows network managers to access a wide 
range of manageable entities in the network for a wide range of 
management tasks.

o An improved routing algorithm (Link State Routing) to provide 
efficient dynamic routing in large networks

The larger a network becomes, the more overhead is generated by 
passing routing information between routers and between routers and 
end systems. Link State routing provides a more efficient algorithm 
for passing routing information while keeping the overhead traffic to a 
minimum.

o Increased addressing to allow networks to grow to potentially include 
millions of nodes

DECnet/OSI uses the OSI standard address format. This format was 
designed to allow each node in a universal network to have a unique 
address. In other words, networks can now grow well beyond the bounds 
of any other addressing format now in use. Existing Phase IV addresses 
can continue to be used for upgraded systems. The Phase IV address is 
moved into the OSI address format.

o Address Autoconfiguration

DECnet/OSI nodes can take advantage of the address autoconfiguration 
feature where the adjacent router configures the node address for you.

o Single Installation/Configuration for OSI components

X.25, the Wide Area Device Drivers, FTAM and Virtual Terminal 
applications are included in the DECnet/OSI H-Kit. All of the 
components are installed and configured together.


In order to fully benefit from these enhancements, changes may need to
be made in the network when upgrading from DECnet-VAX to DECnet/OSI.
Prior to the upgrade, certain decisions need to be made: 

o Network Addressing: Will users on the DECnet/OSI network have the
ability to communicate with users on other OSI networks, either
through electronic mail, EDI, FTAM, VTP or other internetwork
utilities? If yes, it is necessary to obtain a unique network
identifier (IDP) from an authorized authority such as ANSI. If not, a
default IDP is provided with DECnet/OSI which can be used at
installation/configuration time. 

o Naming Services: will naming services be provided locally to each node 
or distributed throughout the network? Larger networks can benefit 
greatly from a distributed naming service. Smaller networks can use a 
local service without impact. 

System Transition
-----------------

In order to upgrade a system from DECnet-VAX to DECnet/OSI, the system 
manager needs to know:

o DECnet Phase IV node name - as for DECnet-VAX
o DECnet/OSI node name - can be derived from the DECnet-VAX name
o DECnet node number - can be derived from the DECnet-VAX address
o Phase IV Address Prefix - default is provided
o Timezone - for the Distributed Time Service

With this information, the system manager can install the DECnet/OSI 
media and run the basic configuration.

With a small network (approximately 150 nodes), transition is simple
and straightforward. Assume that the default IDP will be used and name
services will be local. The DECnet/OSI media is installed using the
standard $VMSINSTAL procedure. The Basic Configuration Procedure is
used and all defaults are accepted. Existing Phase IV name databases
are automatically converted to the local name format and synonym
tables are built to allow the continued use of the familiar 6-character
node names. The system is now running DECnet/OSI with X.25, the WAN 
Drivers and the OSI Applications available for use.

Network level Transition
------------------------

With a larger network, transition is still straightforward - after
some preliminary planning work is complete. The first step is to
obtain an IDP, assuming the network will be open. the second step is
to collect information about the network. Large networks tend to
contain a variety of equipment and applications and an audit is the
best way to find out what's really out there. Since certain older
components have not been upgraded to DNA Phase V they will either have
to be replaced or relegated to a corner of the network. Applications
that depend on older operating system versions will also have to be
evaluated for upgrade or replacement. 

The next step is the namespace. For smaller networks or large networks
where only a few nodes are to be upgraded, it is recommended that the
Local Name Option be used. This allows the network to get up and
running quickly. Should a fully distributed name service be required,
a namespace will need to be designed. (A consulting service is
available to provide assistance in the design of large corporate
namespaces.) The namespace is a hierarchical schema that will
accommodate all network entities that will be contained in the network
directory. This step may require consulting assistance as network
directory design is new to many network managers. Once the schema is
defined, it will be implemented on DECdns servers - local nodes no
longer have to store network information. DECdns servers can be
installed on existing nodes or on dedicated devices. DECnet/OSI can
then be installed using $VMSINSTAL. 

On the backbone, DNA Phase V routers may be installed, or existing
Phase IV routers can continue to be used. DNA Phase V routers can be
configured to run either DNA Phase V or DNA Phase IV routing
protocols. DECnet/OSI is designed to be backwards compatible with
Phase IV to allow transition to happen over time. DECnet/OSI end
systems are compatible with Phase IV routers, and DNA Phase V routers
can understand and work with Phase IV end systems. Level 1 and Level 2
routers can also coexist. 

After end systems are upgraded to DECnet/OSI, DECnet end users will
most likely not even notice that they are working on a new network
stack. Existing Layered Products will continue to run. Network and
system managers will notice as they need to switch from NCP to NCL,
using conversion tools initially to help them over the learning curve.
An NCP emulator is included in the DECnet/OSI kit to allow
applications using NCP to be installed on a system that has upgraded
to NCL. 

Overall, transition to DECnet/OSI is simply an upgrade to new and 
better networking technology. 

Questions & Answers:
--------------------

1) How is DECnet/OSI installed?

DECnet/OSI is a layered product and is installed using VMSINSTAL.

2) Can DECnet-VAX and DECnet/OSI nodes work together in a VAXcluster?

Yes. A VAXcluster can contain both DECnet-VAX and DECnet/OSI nodes. A 
cluster alias may contain all or some of the DECnet-VAX cluster 
members or all/some of the DECnet/OSI cluster members. DECnet/OSI 
allows for the creation of multiple cluster aliases.

3) What are the basic differences between NCL and NCP and what 
commands are needed to get started?

NCL is not required for installation or configuration. There are tools 
shipping with DECnet/OSI that provide assistance with NCL, either by 
generating NCL scripts automatically or providing templates.

In terms of the differences, NCL is based on a hierarchical view of
the network and addresses a wide range of manageable objects. NCP is
based on a flat view. NCL uses a different command structure and does
require a learning curve. However, once the network manager becomes
comfortable with the hierarchy, NCL becomes quite intuitive to use. 

4) How much time will be needed to make this move from DECnet-VAX to 
DECnet/OSI?

Assuming the use of the defaults, an end system can be upgraded within a day 
- including preparation, installation and configuration.

The amount of time required to upgrade the network depends on the size
and complexity of the existing network. Having the backwards
compatibility with DECnet-VAX allows the transition to take place over
a period of time that is comfortable. There are no flag days! 

5) What is the quality of the P.S.I. products in the DECnet/OSI
environment?

The X.25 support provided with DECnet/OSI V5.7 for OpenVMS VAX is an 
upgrade of the standalone P.S.I. products. All past quality enhancements 
to P.S.I. code have been implemented in the DECnet/OSI release.

6) What happens to the existing DECnet Layered Products?

Existing DECnet Layered Products continue to run over DECnet/OSI using 
the DECnet uppers layers. Users of these products will not 
notice a difference in operation after the upgrade has taken place. 

7) If I'm using DECnet-VAX Extensions now, what should I do when I move
to OpenVMS VAX Version 6.0 ?

The DECnet-VAX Extensions have reached the end of their engineering 
life and are not supported on OpenVMS VAX V6.0. DECnet/OSI V5.7 is the 
supported release and users are required to upgrade when moving to 
OpenVMS V6.0.


