In article <15NOV199309585600@almach.caltech.edu>, shoppa@almach.caltech.edu (Timothy D. Shoppa x4256) writes:
> I am trying to bring an old RA80 back to life (to hook to my "home" computer,
> a 11/730 currently residing in my kitchen.  The current configuration
> consists of a 730 with FPA, 4 Megs, IDC with R80 and RL02, 2 DMF32's, a TU80,
> and a UDA50 with a RA60 on it.)

  If it's on an IDC, it isn't an RA80, it's an R80.

> Here is the symptom:
> When I try to spin up the disk, the fault light comes on before there
> is any obvious attempt to start the disk.  Pressing the
> fault button then gives me the following blinking lights:  "Ready", "Write
> Protect", "A", and "B".

  I'm not at work, and I don't know if the R80 faults are the same as the ones
on the RA80. However, since it fails before spinup it could be the belt tension
switch, the brake, or one of the boards.

> Don't some of the RA series drives have a RS-232 interface that
> you can plug a 300 baud terminal into for diagnostics?  I remember the
> socket on a RA82 being under the top lid, near the motor start capacitor.
> I don't see such a jack on my RA80.  However, there are two 4-pin connectors
> under the lid on the back of the unit, labeled "in" and "out".  Is this
> where the diagnostics are supposed to be run from? 

  Most of the RAxx drives have serial ports. The RA80 was pretty primitive,
though (it had two rotary switches for entering commands, which I think were
instead of [as opposed to in addition to] the serial port).

  Your 2 4-pin connectors are for the spindle sequence cables in the RA80
flavor of the drive. They're used to prevent multiple drives in one rack
from spinning up at once. I believe they're unused in the R80.

> Are there any "modern" drives that I can easily hook to my 11/730, and I
> could find surplus?  I see in some of the surplus rags Unibus SCSI controllers,
> but as I understand it I'd have to upgrade to something more modern than VMS4.4
> to talk to a SCSI device.

  Unibus SCSI controllers almost always emulate the DU (UDA-50) device. You
should be able to use one under VMS V4.4 without trouble, as long as you 
already have a DU driver set (meaning they weren't deleted off your disk).

