|>I'm having trouble with VMS V5.5-1 and sharing of STREAMS files.
|>I have two processes accessing a shared file.  I'm using VMS locks
|>to control access to the file.  Unfortunately, changes made by one 
|>process aren't always immediately visible to the other process.
|>The application is in C.  Both processes open the file for
|>shared access.  What good is sharing a file between two processes
|>if changes by one process are not apparent to the other?
|>-- 
|>Pat O'Neil
|>Hughes Network Systems
|>Germantown, Md
|>

The C RTL buffers data.  Assuming you use fflush and fsync
to get the file system coherent, you still have to content with
the C RTL.  I recently made a fix so that fflush flushes the
data it has read, in addition to the data that is pending for
writes.  This change may appear in OpenVMS VAX V6.1 (though it
is not yet in the master source pool).

If you really want to workaround this, you could play with
various fseek() operations.  I'd guess that seeking to the
beginning of the file (or the end) and then seeking back
to your current position would work (as long as the file is
longer than a buffer's worth).  Calling setvbuf might also
have the side effect of flushing the cache.

