Dennis O'Connor <doconnor@sedona.intel.com> wrote:
>
>daniels@biles.com (Brad Daniels) writes:
>] [...] there was no lower limit on order, so any CPU capable of issuing one
>] instruction per clock cycle can be considered to be a superscalar processor
>] of order 1.
>] [...]  To be fair, "common" usage among
>] CPU designers has "superscalar" meaning "superscalar of order >1", 
>
>No, that's not at all correct. "Scalar" processors issue no more than 1
>instruction per cycle; "supercalar" processors issue MORE than that,
>that's why the "super" prefix is there (i.e. "above scalar"). That's
>not "common useage", that's the _definition_. Yes the term "order(n)
>superscalar" is used, but just as an "Order 0" polynomial isn't
>a polynomial at all (it's a constant), "Order 1 superscalar" processsors
>are not superscalar. That all processors have an "order" of
>"superscalarness" does NOT make them all superscalar.

Look, I was arguing the semantics of the thing.  Look at what you just said.
An order 0 polynomial is a constant.  That means a constant is an order 0
polynomial.  Similarly, an order 1 superscalar processor is a scalar
processor, and a scalar processor is an order 1 superscalar processor.
It's a valid, albeit pointless, interpretation.

Now, if I went to an engineering design meeting saying I wanted to make
a new superscalar processor that can retire up to one instruction per
cycle, I'd get laughed out of the room.  If I had a processor that I
wanted to sell, though, I could call it superscalar without violating the
definition of the term.  I worked in sales support for a while, and frequently
got these correct-but-you-sure-don't-want-to-defend-them-to-a-technical-
buyer statements about hardware and software.  Somebody stomped on someone
for calling the 386 superscalar.  I decided to defend the person while at
the same time (I thought) indicating that no reasonable person would use
the term that way.

Lighten up.

