rabagley@deerfly.occ.uc.edu (Ross Bagley) writes:
: Actually any processor with a pipelined architecture is "superscalar" due
: to the fact that it is working on several instructions at once.
: 
: The 80386 is actually considered the most successful superscalar CISC 
: processor to date.  Just so happens that most modern superscalar designs
: use multiple pipelines as well...
: 
: so the 386 the 486 and the pentium are all superscalar...

I do not believe that this is correct.  You are confusing pipelining (ability
to be able to do overlapping execution of instructions) with superscalar.
Superscalar (as mentioned in previous articles) is the ability to issue
multiple instructions to the execution units at the same time.  Superscalar
is an enhancement to a pipelined architecture.

I also believe that the Pentium is the only member of the x86 family to be
superscalar, although others are pipelined.  My expertise on Intel stuff is
slightly limited, however...

John



