dellwo@utkvx.utk.edu (This Side Up) writes:
: When the processor reads an instruction from memory can't it
: read only one instruction per clock cycle. How does a
: superscalar processor execute more than one command per
: cycle. Does the compiler combine commands into one long
: word.
: 
: By the way, I've always called 32 bits a "long word."
: What do you call 64 bits now?  Just curious.
: 
: Thanks, Joe.

There is a corresponding increase in the memory bandwidth that is needed
to support this.  You will typically fetch 2 words/instructions (could be
more) at the same time.  If both (all) of them are able to be issued at
the same time, they are.  An example might be a floating point and integer
instruction fetched simultaneously, then both can be issued together.
This assumes that there is no resource conflict, etc.

John



