If noone is dropping any packets anywhere in your network, then sure, you can
compare their performance on favorable terms.  But I'll echo Dave's remarks that
when you have elements of your network (routers, bridges, slow interface cards, etc.) that are dropping packets,  DECnet Phase IV performance sucks.  It can be
directly traced to the performance of the NSP retransmission algorithm, since at
that point in the DECnet architecture definition work, the slow-start and RTT
variance work that Van Johnson did at LBL (that went into TCP) wasn't known.  So
yes, DECnet Phase IV has performance aspects that are definitely outmoded.

