Well, thank you those who replied to the DECnet performance questions,
both those diehards that I upset, and to those who agreed. 

There were a few useful suggestions, but the bottom line is still that
DECnet does not perform over a routed or bridged network if packets are
dropped. (And I do know that bridges operate at the data link layer, and
I also know they affect DECnet when they drop packets.)

We have made changes to NCP to change factors such as DELAY FACTOR and WEIGHT
but whilst these had an effect on the Suns, they made no difference to the
VAXs. The VAX still waits 4 seconds between retransmissions.

Alphas have a problem over a router. Sorry, but it's true. There are no packets
lost, but the Alpha waits 10 seconds quite regularly. I dunno why.

My own opinion (flame if that's what you enjoy) is that DECnet was designed
in the days when networks consisted of an isolated fast local ethernet, and
slow external nets where long retransmission factors were OK. It doesn't
seem to work too well over routed networks. This is *observation*, and I have
tried many different NCP and router changes. I may not understand DECnet that
well (as one diehard put it), but NG's Expert sniffer agrees with me; and
that kit was made by clever people!
  
Anyhow thanks again for your input, I'd still like to hear from users with 
similiar problems. If you don't want to appear not to be towing the party
line, Email me direct and I`ll not tell.

Dave 

