|> broken, and I don't mean the protocol. Bridges should NOT drop packets.
|> [...]

I think this is way too strong a statement.  Dave's primary problem is that
dropped packets take *way* too long to retransmit in a LAN environment.  This
is primarily due to the RTT estimate in the NSP retransmission algorithm being
kept in *integer* seconds (min=1), leading to default behaviour of 5 sec delay
before NSP will retransmit the lost segment.  One of the approaches to fixing
the problem is to drop the DELAY FACTOR, thus reducing the factor of 5 between
the estimated RTT and the retransmission.  But this is an *end node* parameter,
and must be adjusted for every host that is a source or destination of data.
The networking people can't do that to a router or bridge, or something, and
affect the performance.  And still, you can only reduce, not eliminate, the
excessive delay before retransmission.

As you point out, the best approach to the problem today is to eliminate the
source of the dropped packets.  When the NSP retransmission algorithm was
designed, there weren't bridges (DEC hadn't invented them yet), and it was a
reasonable assumption that Ethernet would be error-free (unless the sender 
detected excessive collision), and that wide area problems would be occuring
on the serial lines, and that DDCMP would handle making those error-free.  
These days, with multi-protocol routers, cheap (not wire speed) bridges,
mismatched LAN speeds, slow network adapters on fast LANS, etc., there's lots
of other places to lose packets, and DECnet really should have had a revision
to the NSP retransmission algorithm.  (yeah, I know, it now has... Phase V :-)

|> I'll end up by pointing out that there is an international DECnet network
|> consisting of tens of thousands of nodes. It contains at least hundreds of
|> bridges and routers and LANs. It seems to run just fine. But I guess Mr.
|> Stafford's observations mean that all the thousands of network users all over
|> the world must be wrong. And remember that small rocks fall slower than big
|> ones, too. People kept telling Galileo that was from observation, too.

Well, since Dave is from ESTEC (previously working at ESOC), I know he's
quite aware of E-SPAN and ESA's part of that network.  But "seems to run just
fine" belies the performance problems that have to get investigated pretty
regularly.  I know I do from the NASA side of things.  I assume that someone
from the ESnet side has to periodically, too.  The reality of modern 
networking is that you do have packet loss in various places, at various 
times.  As service providers, we have to eliminate as much of that as 
possible.  In the meantime, however, DECnet performance takes a *much* bigger
hit than TCP performance, since TCP's retransmission algorithm works well, 
and DECnet (Phase IV) doesn't.

Warren Van Camp
