dstaffor@estec.esa.nl (Dave Stafford x4437) writes:
>Well, thank you those who replied to the DECnet performance questions,
>both those diehards that I upset, and to those who agreed. 
>
>There were a few useful suggestions, but the bottom line is still that
>DECnet does not perform over a routed or bridged network if packets are
>dropped. (And I do know that bridges operate at the data link layer, and
>I also know they affect DECnet when they drop packets.)
>
>We have made changes to NCP to change factors such as DELAY FACTOR and WEIGHT
>but whilst these had an effect on the Suns, they made no difference to the
>VAXs. The VAX still waits 4 seconds between retransmissions.
>
>Alphas have a problem over a router. Sorry, but it's true. There are no packets
>lost, but the Alpha waits 10 seconds quite regularly. I dunno why.
>
>My own opinion (flame if that's what you enjoy) is that DECnet was designed
>in the days when networks consisted of an isolated fast local ethernet, and
>slow external nets where long retransmission factors were OK. It doesn't
>seem to work too well over routed networks. This is *observation*, and I have
>tried many different NCP and router changes. I may not understand DECnet that
>well (as one diehard put it), but NG's Expert sniffer agrees with me; and
>that kit was made by clever people!
>  
>Anyhow thanks again for your input, I'd still like to hear from users with 
>similiar problems. If you don't want to appear not to be towing the party
>line, Email me direct and I`ll not tell.

Let me be charitable. Mr. Stafford has a problem. He doesn't understand the
problem. People who do explain the problem. He feels that it's not worth
understanding, so he just damns the protocol.

I have seen the problems you have seen on many occasions. They are
misconfiguration. I think DEC would do well to examine the defaults for many
parameters. It would make life simpler.

Mr. Stafford talks about bridges dropping packets. This indicates something is
broken, and I don't mean the protocol. Bridges should NOT drop packets.

Mr. Stafford is looking for a scapegoat. He does not know how to fix a problem,
so he simply declares the protocol to be broken and can avoid learning how to
make it work (or paying for the time of someone who does). It will probably
work since DECnet Phase IV is about to be put out to pasture. But to make these
claims is an insult to all of the good engineers who designed the protocol.

I might also mention how many times (starting in about 1970) that people have
pronounced IP as obsolete and unable to perform well on "modern" high speed
networks at, first, 1 MHz and then in about 1980 at 10 MHz, and again in about
1990 at 100 Mhz.

In reality both DECnet and IP are perfectly capable of keeping a 100 MHz pipe
filled. Both do take some configuration changes, though.

I'll end up by pointing out that there is an international DECnet network
consisting of tens of thousands of nodes. It contains at least hundreds of
bridges and routers and LANs. It seems to run just fine. But I guess Mr.
Stafford's observations mean that all the thousands of network users all over
the world must be wrong. And remember that small rocks fall slower than big
ones, too. People kept telling Galileo that was from observation, too.

