In Article <1993Nov11.204536.29025@news.arc.nasa.gov>
vancamp@uchini.arc.nasa.gov (Warren Van Camp) writes:
>In article <2btvn2$qdp@lll-winken.llnl.gov>, oberman@ptavv.llnl.gov writes:
>|> 
>|> Let me be charitable. Mr. Stafford has a problem. He doesn't understand the
>|> problem. People who do explain the problem. He feels that it's not worth
>|> understanding, so he just damns the protocol.
>|> 
>|> I have seen the problems you have seen on many occasions. They are
>|> misconfiguration. I think DEC would do well to examine the defaults for many
>|> parameters. It would make life simpler.
>|> 
>|> Mr. Stafford talks about bridges dropping packets. This indicates something is
>|> broken, and I don't mean the protocol. Bridges should NOT drop packets.
>|> [...]
>
>I think this is way too strong a statement.  Dave's primary problem is that
>dropped packets take *way* too long to retransmit in a LAN environment.  This
>is primarily due to the RTT estimate in the NSP retransmission algorithm being
>kept in *integer* seconds (min=1), leading to default behaviour of 5 sec delay
>before NSP will retransmit the lost segment.  One of the approaches to fixing
>the problem is to drop the DELAY FACTOR, thus reducing the factor of 5 between
>the estimated RTT and the retransmission.  But this is an *end node* parameter,
>and must be adjusted for every host that is a source or destination of data.
>The networking people can't do that to a router or bridge, or something, and
>affect the performance.  And still, you can only reduce, not eliminate, the
>excessive delay before retransmission.

The statement is strong only because Mr. Stafford's statements were phrased in
the context of "This is how it is and I don't care about the truth" mode. Your
description of the problem is only partial, I suspect, because of his
references to problems with an AXP system. I know what an AXP can do to a
network in terms of congestion. The other significant pointer is a repeated
claim that the delay was 4, not 5 seconds. That was quite emphatic. As was the
statement that DELAY FACTOR and DELAY WEIGHT made no difference.

These statements make it look like the real problem may be that the AXP is
simply overflowing the buffers in the Ethernet driver of the VAX by supplying
frames faster than the VAX can process them. As a result DELAY is actually
getting to be GREATER than 1 on the AXP. The answer to this is usually to
adjust the PIPELINE QUOTA on the AXP. More receive buffers on the VAX end might
help, too.

>As you point out, the best approach to the problem today is to eliminate the
>source of the dropped packets.  When the NSP retransmission algorithm was
>designed, there weren't bridges (DEC hadn't invented them yet), and it was a
>reasonable assumption that Ethernet would be error-free (unless the sender 
>detected excessive collision), and that wide area problems would be occuring
>on the serial lines, and that DDCMP would handle making those error-free.  
>These days, with multi-protocol routers, cheap (not wire speed) bridges,
>mismatched LAN speeds, slow network adapters on fast LANS, etc., there's lots
>of other places to lose packets, and DECnet really should have had a revision
>to the NSP retransmission algorithm.  (yeah, I know, it now has... Phase V :-)

Actually there have been revisions in the history of Phase IV, but ever a
change of the integer value of DELAY. I agree this is the root of the problem,
but changing it would require a change in all systems at the same time. Not
trivial. Just as would a change in MAXIMUM BUFFER. I freely admit that DECnet
has slipped behind the times since it was put in maintenance mode.

As to the idea of using <wire speed bridges is something I consider penny wise,
at best. But, even though I don't use them, I realize that there are lots of
them out there.

>|> I'll end up by pointing out that there is an international DECnet network
>|> consisting of tens of thousands of nodes. It contains at least hundreds of
>|> bridges and routers and LANs. It seems to run just fine. But I guess Mr.
>|> Stafford's observations mean that all the thousands of network users all over
>|> the world must be wrong. And remember that small rocks fall slower than big
>|> ones, too. People kept telling Galileo that was from observation, too.
>
>Well, since Dave is from ESTEC (previously working at ESOC), I know he's
>quite aware of E-SPAN and ESA's part of that network.  But "seems to run just
>fine" belies the performance problems that have to get investigated pretty
>regularly.  I know I do from the NASA side of things.  I assume that someone
>from the ESnet side has to periodically, too.  The reality of modern 
>networking is that you do have packet loss in various places, at various 
>times.  As service providers, we have to eliminate as much of that as 
>possible.  In the meantime, however, DECnet performance takes a *much* bigger
>hit than TCP performance, since TCP's retransmission algorithm works well, 
>and DECnet (Phase IV) doesn't.

The algorithm works quite well. It's the implementation detail that messed
things up. Sadly it's simply not worth fixing in a protocol with a 16 bit
address field. (I think that is the only "real" problem.)

IP has excellent support, is more heavily used, and has a pretty poor
application side. I want DECnet over TCP and I don't mean encapsulation,
either. This would provide a common network/transport layer interface and
provide the wonderful programming and RMS interfaces I love DECnet for.

I'll also admit that I read the message just as I came in the door this morning
which is a bad time to see statements that are pretty confrontational. I felt
he was asking for a flame and was in the perfect mood to give him one.

