Andrew,

  One of the difficult issues here is the meaning of the term "supported".
To the system vendor folks (generally speaking) this means something very
different from "it works". To many end users, the issue of whether or not
it works is the primary or only issue for them. Unfortunately, to a system
vendor (virtually any system vendor) the sale of a configured package or
a supported upgrade has both implied/express warranties and liabilities.

  Here in the LSA (Litigious States of America) that carries an extra cost
which requires careful consideration. To DEC, as a specific example, it
means that they've passed DEC STD102 (DEC Standard 102, shock/vibration)
DEC STD103 (FCC/VDE compliance, emissions scans) and a host of others.
Not to mention configuration testing against a variety of other hardware
options and configurations. And, that they are willing to back their
warranty in/on your site. Depending on the companiy's view of the product
volumes and profitability involved, they may choose not to "support" that
particular piece of hardware or configuration. This may be vastly different
than whether or not the hardware "works".

  Having said all this, I too wish that there were simpler mechanisms. I
just happen to be aware of the testing/costs because I used to work there
(so I understand how the trade-offs occur, whether I agree or not).

: (I am aware of a mounting bracket issue, I really mean from an OS point
: of view.)

I hope that helps.

ciao,
