                Novell to use LAT as a Transport Protocol

  SUNNYVALE, Calif.--(BUSINESS WIRE) via First! -- Continuing a strong
commitment to deliver enterprise-wide computing solutions to NetWare
customers, Novell Inc. Tuesday announced NetWare for LAT, a new NetWare-to-
DEC host connectivity product enabling NetWare customers to simultaneously
and transparently access DEC and NetWare services from any desktop, anywhere
on the network.

  NetWare-to-DEC host communications is a part of Novell's commitment to
helping customers rightsize using NetWare and UnixWare for seamless
distributed enterprise computing, as well as AppWare for networked
application development.

  "Currently, customers are successfully using NetWare for SAA and LAN
WorkPlace products to connect to their IBM and UNIX host systems. They have
been asking for DEC connectivity to round out their enterprise-wide
computing needs," said Gerry Machi, vice president and general manager,
Enterprise Systems Group, Novell Inc.  "Netware for LAT is a powerful
communications tool giving customers seamless DEC connectivity, support for
industry standard desktop workstations, comprehensive network management
capabilities and an open applications development environment."

  NetWare for LAT simplifies the installation and administration of
workstation software for NetWare users connecting to DEC hosts. Because
terminal emulators connect to NetWare for LAT using the Novell standard
IPX/SPX or industry standard TCP/IP protocols, customers no longer need to
install LAT or DECnet protocols on each client workstation.  The LAT
connections to the DEC host are secured and managed by NetWare for LAT.

  Netware for LAT also reduces the complexity and management of wide area
connectivity to DEC hosts.  Customers can now use their existing TCP/IP and
IPX/SPX wide area networks to connect to remote DEC hosts, and no longer
need to bridge LAT or route DECnet across their WANs.  NetWare for LAT is
fully integrated with NetWare, leveraging the high performance, security,
scalability and administrative features of the network operating system, and
provides developers an open platform to further integrate DEC and NetWare
services.

  Mike Thompson, IS manager at Integrated Device Technology, Santa Clara, -
a large international semiconductor manufacturing company with 2300
employees, and offices in France, Germany and the Pacific Rim - has been
beta testing NetWare for LAT.  "We're using NetWare for LAT as a dedicated
communications server between our IPX/SPX internetwork and the Vax.  This is
by far the least expensive, most straightforward implementation of a VAX
gateway on the market today.  With the NetWare support of multiple protocols
and hardware adapters, we're able to route any protocol to the VAX through
NetWare for LAT without adding any software to the client workstation or to
the VAX itself.  It's a very clean solution."

  NetWare for LAT, a set of NetWare Loadable modules (NLMS), allows up to
128 simultaneous users access to DEC applications and data over any NetWare
supported topology - including Token-Ring, Ethernet, and Arcnet.  The
product supports third-party terminal emulators for DOS, MS Windows, Apple
Macintosh, OS/2, UNIX, and MS Windows NT workstations, enabling users to
choose the desktop system most appropriate to their needs.

  Users connect to a NetWare for LAT server using IPX/SPX, TCP/IP, or
AppleTalk protocols.  NetWare for LAT in turn provides the protocol
processing, security and management to access multiple DEC hosts.  Built as
a NetWare Loadable Module, NetWare for LAT can run on a dedicated server, or
on an existing server with other NetWare services, and does not require any
special software on the DEC host.

  NetWare for LAT is also an integration platform used by many third-party
vendors, who are developing applications to further integrate the NetWare
and DEC environments.  Over 11 third party vendors announced NetWare for LAT
compatible products today, including products that provide terminal
emulation, file transfer, e-mail services and printing services.

  NetWare for LAT can be installed on NetWare 3.x or 4.x, and can run either
on an existing NetWare server alongside other NetWare services and
applications, or on a dedicated server running NetWare Runtime.  NetWare for
LAT will begin shipping in December, 1993. Suggested list pricing is as
follows: 16 users - $3,495; 32 users - $5,995; 64 users - $9,995; 128 users
- $16,995.  All prices are for the United States and Canada.  U.S. and
Canadian customers may purchase products from Authorized Novell Gold and
Platinum resellers. Customers outside the United States and Canada should
contact their local Novell office.

  For more information on NetWare for LAT and Novell's other enterprise
products, call the Product Information Center of the NetWare Enterprise
Systems Group at 800/NETWARE or 408/747-4000.

