I say Amiga and OS/2 have got it right: case sensitivity of filenames on
display only.  Let me explain why.

Why would you want upper and lower case in a filename?  To make the names
*look* right, e.g.:
        
   D.E.Knuth.refs 
   SunOS-upgrade-plan
   VMScluster.list

But why should you have to type the capitalisation the way it is displayed? 
Only if there was a need for different files whoses names only varied by
the capitalisation of their letters.  I think that this "feature" is
virtually useless; it certainly is not needed to model real world text
(everyone knows that Fred Bloggs and fred bloggs are the same person!). 
And any advantage it might have is dwarfed by the advantages of 

  o  not ever having to use the shift key to type a file name,
  o  not having to specify capitalisation when trying to communicate
     a filename by voice,
  o  being able to change your mind about the case of filename without
     affecting the operation of any software that refers to it.

Multi-case on display only --- the best of both worlds!
         


