I don't know about that.

About 20 years ago I worked inthe instrument repair facility of a large
government research lab (see sig for a clue as to which one) and it was
standard procedure to give any "dirty" equipment a through soap and water
cleaning. This includes averything form 'silly' scopes (mostly Tek 500 and 400
stuff) to DVMs and the like.

Soap and water was about the only solvent that was safe and did not attack some
component (except an occasional problem with paper capacitors). While we did
use a special oven for drying components, the washing was done with commercial
high pressure sprayer and a non-residue soap (Oakite). We sprayed boards down
with soapy water and rinsed with filtered water. From there it was on to the
drying oven for a good baking.

I never want to see another Weston 1240 DVM as long as I live!!!

