I'm not certain what to make of it but on our DECstations running Ultrix 4.x
the "/bin/init" binary has default permissions 755, i.e. it is executable by
users.
	A user noticed this when accidentally executing "init" instead of
"xinit". This got him into an interactive "sh". Exiting from this shell with
CONTROL-D caused "/etc/rc" and consequently "/etc/rc.local" being executed.
Several daemons got started ("/etc/lockd", "nfsd"). While no damage was
caused, all these processes were running happily under this user's UID,
doing whatever they could do with the permissions they had.
	Now, might a security hole be hiding there, or is it just something
to scare an innocent user?

Andreas.
