In article <1993Nov8.153320.7308@mcclb0>, smith@mcclb0.med.nyu.edu writes:

>The prices of CPU boards for 6000-400 machine s have dropped far enough that 
>even we are thinking (slowly) of upgrading our 6410 to a 6420.  I assume many 
>people have thought of this already.  Are there any significant 'gotchas' in 
>doing this that we need to look out for?  The 6410 already has 128Mb memory, 
>so I do not think we need more.  But I am concerned about the licenses: how 
>do we manage these?  We are currently running under CSLG licenses.

CSLG shouldn't post any problems; I don't know if the CSLGs specify a power 
rating, or if they are per-CPU licenses.

3rd party stuff (Diskeeper, I/O Express, MultiNet, PMDF, etc.) will 
require some cash to remain legal.

>The DEC hardware manager said: 'No problem.  We'll stick in the board and 
>it'll come right up'.  This kind of hearty optimism gets me very nervous...

It will come right up, but be sure any privileged stuff you run (CMKRNL,
CMEXEC kind of stuff) will work on an SMP system -- specifically, I had a 
lot of problems (er, "significant system interruptions") caused by the
"SMP compatible" version of WatchDog (an idle process killer).  So I 
switched to WATCHER (which doesn't try to step through the PCBs itself, but 
calls $GETJPI instead) and haven't had a crash caused by my idle process 
killer since then.

So, make sure any DECUS utilities you have will work with SMP systems.  
There are very, very few that *won't*, but you'll certainly notice the ones 
that aren't.

(The problem, by the way, is poor coding -- if a program or device driver 
is coded to the VMS V5.0 specifications for using spinlocks (instead of 
elevated IPL) you "generally" shouldn't have problems with it).

