It looks like the structure is really:

struct foo {
	int m;		/* a field that tracks "mem space"
        struct NODE n;	/* the struct here, not pointer to struct */
 	}

If declared and allocated this way, the compiler will
pad or pack the structure, as you tell it and will never generate
alignment faults.  When declare a pointer:

int *mem;

then "mem + 1" is the address of the next int.  That's not the
same as telling the compiler that the next item is a struct NODE.
By doing the cast, that says that the place where the next int
is supposed to be is really a struct NODE.  That's different
than saying to go to the place where the next struct NODE should be.

When ints are the same size as pointers, both have the same
alignment requirements and so both the packed and padded alignments
of the struct result in the same memory layout.

How to fix it?  Either tell the compiler that your struct NODE
pointer has a base alignment of 4-bytes (via align(4), I think),
or allocate "mem_space" as part of a larger structure, or change
"int *mem_space" to "long int *mem_space".  This last hack gets
back to the sizeof(xxx) = sizeof(pointer) axiom.
