We are considering doing an upgrade from a VAX 6000-520 to a VAX 6000-540.
After getting different answers, both pro and con, I decided to call
Ellen Minter. (Ellen Minder is the coordinator for the CSLG program
at Digital.) According to Ellen, you must buy an OpenVMS Upgrade license
when you add a CPU board to a multiprocessor VAX or AXP system. CSLG's
VAX-VMS or AXP-VMS PAKs would work (on the appropriate system), however, this
policy was enacted so colleges and universities would not have unlicensed
CPU modules, when the systems are sold in the open market (This was DEC
Marketings idea!). In other words, DEC did not want to deal with irate
commericial customers who thought they were buying a fully licenses VAX
6000-540, but ended up with a licensed VAX 6000-520 and a cost of $56000 to
do the license upgrade.

Of course, if the site desires not the license the VAX 6000-420 processor,
then they cannot sell their system as a VAX 6000-420, but as A VAX 6000-410.
The added processor(s) must be sold separately and unlicensed. The previous
statement Ellen did not state as policy and may not be acceptable under CSLG.
The CSLG policies do not cover this "gray" area.

Good Luck!
