It's clear that you don't really understand DECnet, but since DECnet Phase IV
is dead, it's not worth worrying about unless you have a real need. If so,
start looking at where the congestion is occurring and fix it. Look at pipeline
quota (reduce) and receive buffers (increase) for help in this area. Use DEBNIs
on BI systems and DELUAa on Ethernet systems. DELQA+s on Q-Bus ones.

>****** CONSPIRACY THEORY *******
>
>DEC are strangely silent on this issue, or say "But you have Cisco routers,
>and we don't like them." The fact is that DECnet is a "hang-around waiting
>forever" protocol, and IS NO USE as a routed protocol except over slow links.

Poppycock! It works the same over DEC and cisco routers. In fact, cisco did the
firmware for the new DEC Brouters. DEC has been far from silent on the issue.
Maybe DEC does not make the same presentations at DECUS Europe that it makes in
the states? The ones where it says DECnet Phase IV is going away and you'd
better be ready. I've heard it for over 2 years on this side of the pond.

The fact is that DECnet Phase IV is not being updated because DEC has put its
resources in replacing it with OSI. While the wisdom  of this is debateable
today, it was a VERY reasonable choice when the decision was made. It performs
pretty well if properly configured, but I suspect your DECnet test did not have
good configuration. What interfaces ere used? What were the settings of receive
buffers and pipeline quotas?

>
>*********************  SO  ..... ************************
>
>If you have had similiar experiences, or totally diasgree with me, please
>let me know. I've spent a long time looking at this problem, and am tempted
>to conclude that DECnet cannot cut it as a modern protocol.

DECnet specs are readily available and the performance of the protocol is well
established. It is obsolete. DEC is not going to argue this. If you want to
improve things, update to DECnet/OSI when it is available for the Alphas.
DECnet is one of the oldest protocols around today and has worked well for
years, but network technology passed it by very quickly when it was put in
maint. mode several years ago.

