In article <CG0L3x.4wC@wm.estec.esa.nl>, dave@bccsun1.estec.esa.nl (Dave Stafford x4437) writes:
|>Nah, that was a lie to make you read this   :-)

Bad ploy.  Many who have valid input to give to you may skip the message
entirely if all they look at is the subject.

|>What I really want is to hear from anyone who has had problems with DECnet
|>over routers or bridges (whatever the flavour) on a local area network. 
|>
|>****WHY ??****
|>
|>Because I believe that DECnet is an outmoded protocol incapable of responding
|>to the increased bandwidth available in modern networks.

NOT!  Yes, it is beginning to show some age but a proper implementation is
still able to saturate Ethernets with ease and take a large bite out of FDDI
rings.  I've been working at all levels of DECnet and DECnet/OSI for five
years now and, in my opinion, DECnet Phase IV isn't close to being obsolete.
It's major failing is lack of address space.  But then you knew that.

But in terms of performance, there's very little outmoded about it.  IP V4
and DECnet Phase IV are about the same age and suffer from similar problems.
Both can achieve very high performance but performance is very dependent of
the quality of the implementation.

|>Did you know that DECnet (specifically NSP) waits over 4 (YES FOUR!!!) seconds
|>before re-transmitting a packet if an Acknowledgement is dropped by a bridge,
|>router, or the computer itself.

This is dependent on the exec characteristic delay factor.  DECnet/OSI
for ULTRIX and DECnet/OSI for DEC OSF/1 defaults this to 2.  Under
DECnet-ULTRIX, DECnet-VAX and DECnet/OSI for OpenVMS, it defaults to 4.
Change it to 2.  [Note that under Phase IV that delay factor is in units
of 1/16; this means you have to specify 32 to get a value of 2.]

Phase IV:	ncp set exec delay factor 32
Phase V:	ncl set nsp delay factor 2

Also related is the delay weight.  This characteristic controls how much effect
a single packet has on the updating of the round trip time.  The value of 3
it a reasonable value.  Why 3?  Because it works. 

|>**** WHAT !! *****
|>
|>Yes, it's true. "Ah, but change the DELAY FACTOR and DELAY WEIGHT in NCP" you
|>cry. Nope, it doesn't make a blind bit of difference to VAX 9000's or APLHAs.
|>(It does make a huge difference to Suns running DNI though, oops :-)!

It does make a difference whether you believe it or not.

|>***** ALPHAs are slower that 286 PCs *****
|>
|>Yes this is also true. When sending data across a routed FDDI network, the
|>ALPHA achieves a stunning 1.2 Kbits/second! It sends a few packets at 5 Mbps
|>and then has a rest for 10 SECONDS! before doing it again. There seems to be
|>no reason for this, network monitors report that no packets are lost in the 
|>link.

Between a DEC 4000 model 610 and a DEC 3000 model 400 (both with FDDI) I get:

80.00MB bytes (83886080 bytes)
        real = 0.081008u  4.143120s (158.87Mb/s)
        elapsed (23.651%)= 17.860432 (37.57Mb/s)

and this is on a ring with a latency of 300 microseconds and is about
30%-40% utilized.  I'm using DECnet/OSI on DEC OSF/1 AXP.  Yep.  Can
you 286 do that?

|>****** CONSPIRACY THEORY *******
|>
|>DEC are strangely silent on this issue, or say "But you have Cisco routers,
|>and we don't like them." The fact is that DECnet is a "hang-around waiting
|>forever" protocol, and IS NO USE as a routed protocol except over slow links.

Although I don't speak officially for DEC (like they would really want
me to), I know that I have in the past responded via news and mail to
you about the problems you are having.

I know of at least one bug of Cisco's that we had to work around in the
ULTRIX DECnet implementation (the Cisco router would insert an additional
datalink header before the DECnet packet thereby causing a lost packet).

|>*********************  SO  ..... ************************
|>
|>If you have had similiar experiences, or totally diasgree with me, please
|>let me know. I've spent a long time looking at this problem, and am tempted
|>to conclude that DECnet cannot cut it as a modern protocol.

In conclusion, I conclude that your conclusion would be in error if you
