In article <TWILLEY.93Oct28165548@dewey.nl.nuwc.navy.mil>, twilley@dewey.nl.nuwc.navy.mil (Jack Twilley) writes:
> Anyone know how I can get microsecond resolution under VMS?

You can get time values to microseconds, but you can't use the VAX clock.
We do this by using special-purpose hardware and locally developed software.

To do this, you will need to purchase a GPS-based satellite time code
receiver and a time code generator card for your VAX.  The satellite
receiver locks onto a timing signal from one or more of the GPS satellites,
and outputs an 'IRIG-A' or 'IRIG-B' analog time code signal to one or more
VAXen.  The VAX time code generator card uses the IRIG signal to produce
various accurate, high-resolution time values in output registers.  Your
software can then read these time values from the card registers.

Unfortunately, your hardware budget will take a hit.  Your total cost may
run anywhere from $2000 to $4000+, depending on the vendor, desired features,
and type of VAX.

Hope this helps....

