>Does anybody know why ESC is not a key by itself, like TAB or DELETE on
>DEC keyboards? It is extremely annoying for people who are occasional
>users of these keyboards.

Actually, the new LK-401 keyboards have an (ESC) key, but the default setting
is to have it send an accent grave. The reason is DEC's occasionally silly
adherence to international standards.

Get a copy of the ISO standard for keyboard layout. It does NOT say what keys
should be located where, but does describe the general layout of the keyboard
as is seen on the DEC LK201, LK401, The Apple Mac Extended Keyboard and the IBM
Extended Keyboard. It suggests that the function keys be placed in a row at the
top with the keys divided into three sections, alpha-numerics, editing, and
numeric. I think this was "A GOOD THING", but it also recommended that no key
except for <RET> and <HT> be placed on the keyboard which generates a control
character (ASCII value <32 is control). Thus DEC pulled the <LF>, <BS>, and
<ESC> keys.

Or so I was told by a member of the DEC terminal group back when the VT-200 was
announced.

