COMP.SYS.DEC

Thanks to all of you who responded to my earlier post.  The general 
consensus was that some form of alcohol (isopropyl and ethyl were both 
mentioned) on a rag or paper towel would be best.  Other suggestions were
Windex and Fantastik.

Here's a summary of my "experimental results" (and please don't jump on me
for the lack of controls and so forth).  I've just cleaned one or more of
each of the following: 

  A.  Apple Extended Keyboard II
  B.  VAXStation 3100 M38 Keyboard
  C.  Sparc 1 Keyboard
  D.  Personal Iris Keyboard
  E.  Iris Indigo Keyboard

Using:
  A.  95% Ethyl Alcohol
  B.  409  (closest thing to Fantastik in our stockroom)
  C.  Windex

On:
  1.  Kimwipes (the big ones)
  2.  Scotch 150 towels (the white ones - like in restroom dispensers)
  3.  Cotton Tipped wooden swabs

First result, some keyboards are a *lot* easier to clean than others.  Some
kinds of plastic come clean with a wipe or two, others have to be scrubbed.
Texturing in the plastic tends to trap dirt.  Lastly, the shape of the keys
makes a big difference - some you can just wipe between and others have to
be laboriously cleaned one by one with swabs.  From most cleanable to least
the order was: 

  D, [C, E], A,            B

That's right, the DEC keyboard was the most difficult to clean by a wide 
margin.  I never did get all the dirt out of the deep crannies in its
textured surface.  In comparison, the Personal Iris keyboard was a breeze,
and it was the dirtiest of the lot to start with. 

Which solvent to use?  For most kinds of dirt ethanol worked best. There
were a few brown spots on one keyboard that looked like dried coca-cola
which would not come off with ethanol, but yielded immediately to either
409 or Windex.  This might not be a problem with more dilute ethanol.  
In all cases solvent was applied to the cleaning paper/swab, not directly
to the keyboard!  409 tended to foam up if it was scrubbed, so it had to be
used sparingly in order to keep it out of the keyboard. 

None of the 3 cleaning materials was really optimal: the kimwipes were much
less effective than the paper towels;  the paper towels tended to shred
when scrubbing keys; and the Cotton swabs were good for hard to reach spots
but impractical for doing a whole keyboard.  In the end I used the paper
towels for almost everything.  Often a bit of paper towel wrapped around
the swab worked better than the swab alone. 

Happy cleaning,

David Mathog
mathog@seqvax.bio.caltech.edu


