In article <1993Oct29.194528.4348@merlin.dev.cdx.mot.com>, lezz@merlin.dev.cdx.mot.com (Lezz Giles) writes:
|>
|>Ultrix on OSF offers me a smooth upgrade path from MIPS.  It would
|>almost certainly increase the number of vendors supporting tools on
|>MIPS/Ultrix (this number is currently dropping very quickly).  It
|>offers me the chance of having a network with a mix of MIPS/Ultrix and
|>Alpha/Ultrix without too many compatibility headaches (and therefore
|>the chance to slowly move the network over to the new architecture).
|>
|>Lezz
|>

What gives you the idea that ULTRIX on Alpha AXP will be "easier" and more popular? 

People have been slamming Digital about ULTRIX for years, so why should we perpetuate the pain with an
Alpha port?

By the time we put the effort into what was needed to get ULTRIX up to a "modern UNIX" in people's minds, we
would have ended up with something like OSF/1.

It has been shown time and time again that there are not a lot of "technical" hurdles to getting code
over to DEC OSF/1 systems - we engineered the compatiblity into the OS without having to resort to 
"compatiblity boxes" like Slowaris 2. There are references from ISV's and Digital folks who've done
ports. I do admit that the biggest gotchas are poorly written code that makes architecture
specific assumptions (sizeof(*) == sizeof(int) and big-endian byte arrangements for example).

ULTRIX and DEC OSF/1 boxes work just fine on the network together (and with HP and IBM, etc). I and several 
customers are running them. You're not changing the architecture of
the network, you're changing the architecture of a system running on it. Network interoperability is like
Prego spaghetti sauce, it's in there.

ISV's that use technical difficulty arguments to port to DEC OSF/1 are trying to justify
a business decision NOT to do the port. I maintain that lack of OSF/1 port is a business decision because
ISV's don't see the business coming from Digital platforms yet. And yes, this is probably due to Digital's
track record in the UNIX market to date.

I doubt that an ULTRIX port to Alpha would make any difference to ISV's. They'd still need to create a new
version for an Alpha ULTRIX, and you'd have the same business arguments as they are giving about the
lack of OSF/1 ports.

By the way, ask all those SunOS vendors why they haven't ported to Slowaris 2. Now *there's* a technical reason
why there's no applications.

Back to the original premise: VMS vs UNIX:

Outside of the religious wars of operating systems, remember that nobody ever solved a business
problem with an operating system.

My advice to the original poster is: If you like VMS, and the application that you have, *and* the appliation
you want is ported to OpenVMS AXP (OpenVMS IS VMS on both VAX and Alpha platforms) and any of a number of
valid reasons (warm and fuzzies included). - then stay with VMS. Don't be pressured to go off VMS just to go 
to UNIX. 

However, if for any number of equally valid reasons you believe that UNIX systems are better for your
organization, then by all means, consider the move to UNIX - and not just HP. Look at Sun, HP, Digital,
IBM, SGI, etc. Look at what they all offer you - today and future positioning. Then make your decision.

Bottom line: You make the decision based on what's best for your business - applications, training your
people, and growth, etc. Not whether VMS or UNIX is better.

Oh, and by the way. I don't speak VMS much at all.

OS bashing isn't fashionable any more.

