|> and so it goes on and on.  It never stops, but it would be *real* nice
|> if it slowed down a little now and then.
|>
|>  You spend all your time upgrading and upgrading and you don't get any
|> real work done and then when you think you're done upgrading, well then your
|> hardware is really obsolete and you have to start all over again because
|> you can't get parts and service and it won't interface to the rest of 
|> the world anymore anyway.
|>
|>    The industry sure has us by the short hairs, doesn't it?
|>
|>   I see more and more complaints from users.  Magazines are now featuring
|> articles on upgrade mania and its resultant upgrade sicknesses.  Pretty soon
|> the shrinks will have a name for this new family of diseases.  Upgradicitis?
|> Obsolemania?  DEC-o-phobia?    My boss suggested "Future Shock" syndrome.
|>
|>    Got any more disease-name suggestions?
|>

I'm not too sure how serious you're being... but upgrading is a fact of life.
I work on a product with a very long life-cycle between releases (our
first release took _way_ too long, and our second release may take at least
one or two years).  The product is also in a competative market - we have
several competitors that we would like to stay ahead of.  Therefore we need
to reduce our cycle time.  Now there are several ways to do this - including
getting better tools, or improving our development process, but one of our
options is to get the fastest workstations that we can (obviously they
must support the tools that we use).  When you think that it takes over 24
hours to fully build our product on DECstations, you can begin to realize what
sort of improvement we'd get in development time if we could improve that
by an order of magnitude.  To put it simply, we can't afford to ignore
improvements in speed and technology.

Of course, you always need to balance the need for more speed with the need
for stability, but to pretend that machines that are three years old are
just as good as this years machines is ridiculous - if you need power, then
you need to upgrade.

Lezz


