|>You mean on Alpha AXP?  How much smoother would you like the 
|>software upgrade path to be?   You can already recompile or 
|>translate - it's a no-brainer in virtually every case (most 
|>of the problem cases are with non-32 bit clean code).

Our important tools are not home-grown, but come from outside vendors..

|>>It would
|>>almost certainly increase the number of vendors supporting tools on
|>>MIPS/Ultrix (this number is currently dropping very quickly).  
|>
|>Possibly.  They would still be supporting an old platform and a
|>new platform for which images are not directly compatible and so
|>would have to recompile and requalify software.  So you would still
|>see some dropoff, especially as the switch-over is easy.  

See above.

|>>It
|>>offers me the chance of having a network with a mix of MIPS/Ultrix and
|>>Alpha/Ultrix without too many compatibility headaches (and therefore
|>>the chance to slowly move the network over to the new architecture).
|>
|>My group is doing that now, without much in the way of headache. 
|>We have about even numbers of MIPS/Ultrix and DEC OSF/1 Alpha machines
|>all of which are fairly tightly integrated (e.g. shared disks, printers,
|>and so on).   All are used for software development.

This sounds reasonable.  However, there is another consideration which
is driving us towards Sun or HP, and which burned us with DECstations -
we found that it is worth-while using the platform of choice of the tools
vendor.  You're more likely to get quick support, you're going to get
new releases sooner, and you're more likely to get your bugs fixed.
Unfortunately (for DEC at least) the most common platform-of-choice seems
to be Sun.  I must admit that if all the vendors said that they were going
to move their primary development over to Alpha then things would be
different.

